         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doeptsiz10</name>
     <description>The application must modify this register before enabling the endpoint. Once the endpoint is enabled using Endpoint Enable bit of the Device Endpoint-n Control registers (DIEPCTLn.EPEna/DOEPCTLn.EPEna), the core modifies this register. The application can only read this register once the core has cleared the Endpoint Enable bit.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc50</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>xfersize</name>
       <description>Indicates the transfer size in bytes for endpoint 0. The core interrupts the application only after it has exhausted the transfer size amount of data. The transfer size can be Set to the maximum packet size of the endpoint, to be interrupted at the end of each packet. The core decrements this field every time a packet from the external memory is written to the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>18</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>pktcnt</name>
       <description>Indicates the total number of USB packets that constitute the Transfer Size amount of data for endpoint 0.This field is decremented every time a packet (maximum size or short packet) is read from the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>19</lsb>
       <msb>28</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>rxdpid</name>
       <description>Applies to isochronous OUT endpoints only.This is the data PID received in the last packet for this endpoint. Use datax.
Applies to control OUT Endpoints only. Use packetx. This field specifies the number of back-to-back SETUP data packets the endpoint can receive.</description>
       <lsb>29</lsb>
       <msb>30</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doeptsiz10_rxdpid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data0</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data2packet1</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data1packet2</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>mdatapacket3</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdma10</name>
     <description>DMA OUT Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc54</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdma10</name>
       <description>Holds the start address of the external memory for storing or fetching endpoint data. for control endpoints, this field stores control OUT data packets as well as SETUP transaction data packets. When more than three SETUP packets are received back-to-back, the SETUP data packet in the memory is overwritten. This register is incremented on every AHB transaction. The application can give only a DWORD-aligned address.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is not enabled, the application programs the start address value in this field.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field indicates the base pointer for the descriptor list.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdmab10</name>
     <description>DMA Buffer Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc5c</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdmab10</name>
       <description>Holds the current buffer address. This register is updated as and when the data transfer for the corresponding end point is in progress. This register is present only in Scatter/Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepctl11</name>
     <description>Out Endpoint 11.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc60</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>mps</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. The application must program this field with the maximum packet size for the current logical endpoint. This value is in bytes.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>10</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>usbactep</name>
       <description>Indicates whether this endpoint is active in the current configuration and interface. The core clears this bit for all endpoints (other than EP 0) after detecting a USB reset. After receiving the SetConfiguration and SetInterface commands, the application must program endpoint registers accordingly and set this bit.</description>
       <lsb>15</lsb>
       <msb>15</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl11_usbactep_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>dpid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Contains the PID of the packet to be received or transmitted on this endpoint. The application must program the PID of the first packet to be received or transmitted on this endpoint, after the endpoint is activated. The applications use the SetD1PID and SetD0PID fields of this register to program either DATA0 or DATA1 PID.
0: DATA0
1: DATA1This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode. Even/Odd (Micro)Frame (EO_FrNum) In non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode:
Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Indicates the (micro)frame number in which the core transmits/receives isochronous data for this endpoint. The application must program the even/odd (micro) frame number in which it intends to transmit/receive isochronous data for this endpoint using the SetEvnFr and SetOddFr fields in this register.
0: Even (micro)frame
1: Odd (micro)frame
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field is reserved. The frame number in which to send data is provided in the transmit descriptor structure. The frame in which data is received is updated in receive descriptor structure.</description>
       <lsb>16</lsb>
       <msb>16</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl11_dpid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>naksts</name>
       <description>When either the application or the core sets this bit:
-The core stops receiving any data on an OUT endpoint, even if there is space in the RxFIFO to accommodate the incoming packet.
-for non-isochronous IN endpoints: The core stops transmitting any data on an IN endpoint, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
-for isochronous IN endpoints: The core sends out a zero-length data packet, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
       <lsb>17</lsb>
       <msb>17</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl11_naksts_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>nonnak</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>nak</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>eptype</name>
       <description>This is the transfer type supported by this logical endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>18</lsb>
       <msb>19</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl11_eptype_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>control</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>isochronous</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>bulk</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>interrup</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>snp</name>
       <description>Applies to OUT endpoints only. This bit configures the endpoint to Snoop mode. In Snoop mode, the core does not check the correctness of OUT packets before transferring them to application memory.</description>
       <lsb>20</lsb>
       <msb>20</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl11_snp_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disable</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enable</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>stall</name>
       <description>Applies to non-control, non-isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. The application sets this bit to stall all tokens from the USB host to this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Only the application can clear this bit, never the core. Applies to control endpoints only. The application can only set this bit, and the core clears it, when a SETUP token is received for this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
       <lsb>21</lsb>
       <msb>21</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl11_stall_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>cnak</name>
       <description>A write to this bit clears the NAK bit for the endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>26</lsb>
       <msb>26</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl11_cnak_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>snak</name>
       <description>A write to this bit sets the NAK bit for the endpoint. Using this bit, the application can control the transmission of NAK handshakes on an endpoint. The core can also Set this bit for an endpoint after a SETUP packet is received on that endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>27</lsb>
       <msb>27</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl11_snak_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setd0pid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA0. This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode. In non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode: Set Even (micro)frame (SetEvenFr) Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Even/Odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to even (micro) frame. When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field is reserved. The frame number in which to send data is in the transmit descriptor structure. The frame in which to receive data is updated in receive descriptor structure.</description>
       <lsb>28</lsb>
       <msb>28</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl11_setd0pid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setd1pid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA1. This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode.
Set Odd (micro)frame (SetOddFr) Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Even/Odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to odd (micro)frame.This field is not applicable for Scatter/Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>29</lsb>
       <msb>29</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl11_setd1pid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epdis</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. The application sets this bit to stop transmitting/receiving data on an endpoint, even before the transfer for that endpoint is complete. The application must wait for the Endpoint Disabled interrupt before treating the endpoint as disabled. The core clears this bit before setting the Endpoint Disabled interrupt. The application must set this bit only if Endpoint Enable is already set for this endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>30</lsb>
       <msb>30</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl11_epdis_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epena</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.
-When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled,
-for IN endpoints this bit indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer with data ready to transmit is setup.
-for OUT endpoint it indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer to receive data is setup.
-When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled such as for buffer-pointer based DMA mode:
- for IN endpoints, this bit indicates that data is ready to be transmitted on the endpoint.
- for OUT endpoints, this bit indicates that the application has allocated the memory to start receiving data from the USB.
- The core clears this bit before setting any of the following interrupts on this endpoint:
-SETUP Phase Done
-Endpoint Disabled
-Transfer Completed
for control endpoints in DMA mode, this bit must be set to be able to transfer SETUP data packets in memory.</description>
       <lsb>31</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl11_epena_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepint11</name>
     <description>This register indicates the status of an endpoint with respect to USB- and AHB-related events. The application must read this register when the OUT Endpoints Interrupt bit or IN Endpoints Interrupt bit of the Core Interrupt register (GINTSTS.OEPInt or GINTSTS.IEPInt, respectively) is set. Before the application can read this register, it must first read the Device All Endpoints Interrupt (DAINT) register to get the exact endpoint number for the Device Endpoint-n Interrupt register. The application must clear the appropriate bit in this register to clear the corresponding bits in the DAINT and GINTSTS registers.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc68</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>xfercompl</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled
This field indicates that the requested data from the internal FIFO is moved to external system memory. This interrupt is generated only when the corresponding endpoint descriptor is closed, and the IOC bit for the corresponding descriptor is Set.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is disabled, this field indicates that the programmed transfer is complete on the AHB as well as on the USB, for this endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint11_xfercompl_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epdisbld</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. This bit indicates that the endpoint is disabled per the application's request.</description>
       <lsb>1</lsb>
       <msb>1</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint11_epdisbld_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>ahberr</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.This is generated only in Internal DMA mode when there is an AHB error during an AHB read/write. The application can read the corresponding endpoint DMA address register to get the error address.</description>
       <lsb>2</lsb>
       <msb>2</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint11_ahberr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setup</name>
       <description>Applies to control OUT endpoints only. Indicates that the SETUP phase for the control endpoint is complete and no more back-to-back SETUP packets were received for the current control transfer. On this interrupt, the application can decode the received SETUP data packet.</description>
       <lsb>3</lsb>
       <msb>3</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint11_setup_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>outtknepdis</name>
       <description>Applies only to control OUT endpoints. Indicates that an OUT token was received when the endpoint was not yet enabled. This interrupt is asserted on the endpoint for which the OUT token was received.</description>
       <lsb>4</lsb>
       <msb>4</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint11_outtknepdis_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>stsphsercvd</name>
       <description>This interrupt is valid only for Control OUT endpoints and only in Scatter Gather DMA mode. This interrupt is generated only after the core has transferred all the data that the host has sent during the data phase of a control write transfer, to the system memory buffer. The interrupt indicates to the application that the host has switched from data phase to the status phase of a Control Write transfer. The application can use this interrupt to ACK or STALL the Status phase, after it has decoded the data phase. This is applicable only in Case of Scatter Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>5</lsb>
       <msb>5</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint11_stsphsercvd_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>back2backsetup</name>
       <description>Applies to Control OUT endpoints only. This bit indicates that the core has received more than three back-to-back SETUP packets for this particular endpoint. for information about handling this interrupt,</description>
       <lsb>6</lsb>
       <msb>6</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint11_back2backsetup_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>outpkterr</name>
       <description>Applies to OUT endpoints Only This interrupt is asserted when the core detects an overflow or a CRC error for non-Isochronous OUT packet.</description>
       <lsb>8</lsb>
       <msb>8</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint11_outpkterr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bnaintr</name>
       <description>This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled. The core generates this interrupt when the descriptor accessed is not ready for the Core to process, such as Host busy or DMA done</description>
       <lsb>9</lsb>
       <msb>9</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint11_bnaintr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>pktdrpsts</name>
       <description>This bit indicates to the application that an ISOC OUT packet has been dropped. This bit does not have an associated mask bit and does not generate an interrupt.</description>
       <lsb>11</lsb>
       <msb>11</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint11_pktdrpsts_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bbleerr</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when babble is received for the endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>12</lsb>
       <msb>12</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint11_bbleerr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>nakintrpt</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when a NAK is transmitted or received by the device. In case of isochronous IN endpoints the interrupt gets generated when a zero length packet is transmitted due to un-availability of data in the TXFifo.</description>
       <lsb>13</lsb>
       <msb>13</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint11_nakintrpt_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>nyetintrpt</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when a NYET response is transmitted for a non isochronous OUT endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>14</lsb>
       <msb>14</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint11_nyetintrpt_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doeptsiz11</name>
     <description>The application must modify this register before enabling the endpoint. Once the endpoint is enabled using Endpoint Enable bit of the Device Endpoint-n Control registers (DIEPCTLn.EPEna/DOEPCTLn.EPEna), the core modifies this register. The application can only read this register once the core has cleared the Endpoint Enable bit.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc70</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>xfersize</name>
       <description>Indicates the transfer size in bytes for endpoint 0. The core interrupts the application only after it has exhausted the transfer size amount of data. The transfer size can be Set to the maximum packet size of the endpoint, to be interrupted at the end of each packet. The core decrements this field every time a packet from the external memory is written to the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>18</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>pktcnt</name>
       <description>Indicates the total number of USB packets that constitute the Transfer Size amount of data for endpoint 0.This field is decremented every time a packet (maximum size or short packet) is read from the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>19</lsb>
       <msb>28</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>rxdpid</name>
       <description>Applies to isochronous OUT endpoints only.This is the data PID received in the last packet for this endpoint. Use datax.
Applies to control OUT Endpoints only. Use packetx. This field specifies the number of back-to-back SETUP data packets the endpoint can receive.</description>
       <lsb>29</lsb>
       <msb>30</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doeptsiz11_rxdpid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data0</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data2packet1</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data1packet2</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>mdatapacket3</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdma11</name>
     <description>DMA OUT Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc74</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdma11</name>
       <description>Holds the start address of the external memory for storing or fetching endpoint data. for control endpoints, this field stores control OUT data packets as well as SETUP transaction data packets. When more than three SETUP packets are received back-to-back, the SETUP data packet in the memory is overwritten. This register is incremented on every AHB transaction. The application can give only a DWORD-aligned address.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is not enabled, the application programs the start address value in this field.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field indicates the base pointer for the descriptor list.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdmab11</name>
     <description>DMA Buffer Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc7c</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdmab11</name>
       <description>Holds the current buffer address. This register is updated as and when the data transfer for the corresponding end point is in progress. This register is present only in Scatter/Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepctl12</name>
     <description>Out Endpoint 12.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc80</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>mps</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. The application must program this field with the maximum packet size for the current logical endpoint. This value is in bytes.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>10</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>usbactep</name>
       <description>Indicates whether this endpoint is active in the current configuration and interface. The core clears this bit for all endpoints (other than EP 0) after detecting a USB reset. After receiving the SetConfiguration and SetInterface commands, the application must program endpoint registers accordingly and set this bit.</description>
       <lsb>15</lsb>
       <msb>15</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl12_usbactep_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>dpid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Contains the PID of the packet to be received or transmitted on this endpoint. The application must program the PID of the first packet to be received or transmitted on this endpoint, after the endpoint is activated. The applications use the SetD1PID and SetD0PID fields of this register to program either DATA0 or DATA1 PID.
0: DATA0
1: DATA1This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode. Even/Odd (Micro)Frame (EO_FrNum) In non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode:
Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Indicates the (micro)frame number in which the core transmits/receives isochronous data for this endpoint. The application must program the even/odd (micro) frame number in which it intends to transmit/receive isochronous data for this endpoint using the SetEvnFr and SetOddFr fields in this register.
0: Even (micro)frame
1: Odd (micro)frame
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field is reserved. The frame number in which to send data is provided in the transmit descriptor structure. The frame in which data is received is updated in receive descriptor structure.</description>
       <lsb>16</lsb>
       <msb>16</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl12_dpid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>naksts</name>
       <description>When either the application or the core sets this bit:
-The core stops receiving any data on an OUT endpoint, even if there is space in the RxFIFO to accommodate the incoming packet.
-for non-isochronous IN endpoints: The core stops transmitting any data on an IN endpoint, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
-for isochronous IN endpoints: The core sends out a zero-length data packet, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
       <lsb>17</lsb>
       <msb>17</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl12_naksts_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>nonnak</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>nak</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>eptype</name>
       <description>This is the transfer type supported by this logical endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>18</lsb>
       <msb>19</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl12_eptype_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>control</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>isochronous</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>bulk</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>interrup</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>snp</name>
       <description>Applies to OUT endpoints only. This bit configures the endpoint to Snoop mode. In Snoop mode, the core does not check the correctness of OUT packets before transferring them to application memory.</description>
       <lsb>20</lsb>
       <msb>20</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl12_snp_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disable</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enable</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>stall</name>
       <description>Applies to non-control, non-isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. The application sets this bit to stall all tokens from the USB host to this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Only the application can clear this bit, never the core. Applies to control endpoints only. The application can only set this bit, and the core clears it, when a SETUP token is received for this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
       <lsb>21</lsb>
       <msb>21</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl12_stall_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>cnak</name>
       <description>A write to this bit clears the NAK bit for the endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>26</lsb>
       <msb>26</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl12_cnak_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>snak</name>
       <description>A write to this bit sets the NAK bit for the endpoint. Using this bit, the application can control the transmission of NAK handshakes on an endpoint. The core can also Set this bit for an endpoint after a SETUP packet is received on that endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>27</lsb>
       <msb>27</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl12_snak_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setd0pid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA0. This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode. In non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode: Set Even (micro)frame (SetEvenFr) Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Even/Odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to even (micro) frame. When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field is reserved. The frame number in which to send data is in the transmit descriptor structure. The frame in which to receive data is updated in receive descriptor structure.</description>
       <lsb>28</lsb>
       <msb>28</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl12_setd0pid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setd1pid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA1. This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode.
Set Odd (micro)frame (SetOddFr) Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Even/Odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to odd (micro)frame.This field is not applicable for Scatter/Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>29</lsb>
       <msb>29</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl12_setd1pid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epdis</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. The application sets this bit to stop transmitting/receiving data on an endpoint, even before the transfer for that endpoint is complete. The application must wait for the Endpoint Disabled interrupt before treating the endpoint as disabled. The core clears this bit before setting the Endpoint Disabled interrupt. The application must set this bit only if Endpoint Enable is already set for this endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>30</lsb>
       <msb>30</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl12_epdis_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epena</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.
-When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled,
-for IN endpoints this bit indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer with data ready to transmit is setup.
-for OUT endpoint it indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer to receive data is setup.
-When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled such as for buffer-pointer based DMA mode:
- for IN endpoints, this bit indicates that data is ready to be transmitted on the endpoint.
- for OUT endpoints, this bit indicates that the application has allocated the memory to start receiving data from the USB.
- The core clears this bit before setting any of the following interrupts on this endpoint:
-SETUP Phase Done
-Endpoint Disabled
-Transfer Completed
for control endpoints in DMA mode, this bit must be set to be able to transfer SETUP data packets in memory.</description>
       <lsb>31</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl12_epena_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepint12</name>
     <description>This register indicates the status of an endpoint with respect to USB- and AHB-related events. The application must read this register when the OUT Endpoints Interrupt bit or IN Endpoints Interrupt bit of the Core Interrupt register (GINTSTS.OEPInt or GINTSTS.IEPInt, respectively) is set. Before the application can read this register, it must first read the Device All Endpoints Interrupt (DAINT) register to get the exact endpoint number for the Device Endpoint-n Interrupt register. The application must clear the appropriate bit in this register to clear the corresponding bits in the DAINT and GINTSTS registers.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc88</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>xfercompl</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled
This field indicates that the requested data from the internal FIFO is moved to external system memory. This interrupt is generated only when the corresponding endpoint descriptor is closed, and the IOC bit for the corresponding descriptor is Set.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is disabled, this field indicates that the programmed transfer is complete on the AHB as well as on the USB, for this endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint12_xfercompl_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epdisbld</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. This bit indicates that the endpoint is disabled per the application's request.</description>
       <lsb>1</lsb>
       <msb>1</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint12_epdisbld_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>ahberr</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.This is generated only in Internal DMA mode when there is an AHB error during an AHB read/write. The application can read the corresponding endpoint DMA address register to get the error address.</description>
       <lsb>2</lsb>
       <msb>2</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint12_ahberr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setup</name>
       <description>Applies to control OUT endpoints only. Indicates that the SETUP phase for the control endpoint is complete and no more back-to-back SETUP packets were received for the current control transfer. On this interrupt, the application can decode the received SETUP data packet.</description>
       <lsb>3</lsb>
       <msb>3</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint12_setup_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>outtknepdis</name>
       <description>Applies only to control OUT endpoints. Indicates that an OUT token was received when the endpoint was not yet enabled. This interrupt is asserted on the endpoint for which the OUT token was received.</description>
       <lsb>4</lsb>
       <msb>4</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint12_outtknepdis_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>stsphsercvd</name>
       <description>This interrupt is valid only for Control OUT endpoints and only in Scatter Gather DMA mode. This interrupt is generated only after the core has transferred all the data that the host has sent during the data phase of a control write transfer, to the system memory buffer. The interrupt indicates to the application that the host has switched from data phase to the status phase of a Control Write transfer. The application can use this interrupt to ACK or STALL the Status phase, after it has decoded the data phase. This is applicable only in Case of Scatter Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>5</lsb>
       <msb>5</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint12_stsphsercvd_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>back2backsetup</name>
       <description>Applies to Control OUT endpoints only. This bit indicates that the core has received more than three back-to-back SETUP packets for this particular endpoint. for information about handling this interrupt,</description>
       <lsb>6</lsb>
       <msb>6</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint12_back2backsetup_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>outpkterr</name>
       <description>Applies to OUT endpoints Only This interrupt is asserted when the core detects an overflow or a CRC error for non-Isochronous OUT packet.</description>
       <lsb>8</lsb>
       <msb>8</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint12_outpkterr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bnaintr</name>
       <description>This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled. The core generates this interrupt when the descriptor accessed is not ready for the Core to process, such as Host busy or DMA done</description>
       <lsb>9</lsb>
       <msb>9</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint12_bnaintr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>pktdrpsts</name>
       <description>This bit indicates to the application that an ISOC OUT packet has been dropped. This bit does not have an associated mask bit and does not generate an interrupt.</description>
       <lsb>11</lsb>
       <msb>11</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint12_pktdrpsts_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bbleerr</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when babble is received for the endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>12</lsb>
       <msb>12</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint12_bbleerr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>nakintrpt</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when a NAK is transmitted or received by the device. In case of isochronous IN endpoints the interrupt gets generated when a zero length packet is transmitted due to un-availability of data in the TXFifo.</description>
       <lsb>13</lsb>
       <msb>13</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint12_nakintrpt_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>nyetintrpt</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when a NYET response is transmitted for a non isochronous OUT endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>14</lsb>
       <msb>14</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint12_nyetintrpt_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doeptsiz12</name>
     <description>The application must modify this register before enabling the endpoint. Once the endpoint is enabled using Endpoint Enable bit of the Device Endpoint-n Control registers (DIEPCTLn.EPEna/DOEPCTLn.EPEna), the core modifies this register. The application can only read this register once the core has cleared the Endpoint Enable bit.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc90</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>xfersize</name>
       <description>Indicates the transfer size in bytes for endpoint 0. The core interrupts the application only after it has exhausted the transfer size amount of data. The transfer size can be Set to the maximum packet size of the endpoint, to be interrupted at the end of each packet. The core decrements this field every time a packet from the external memory is written to the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>18</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>pktcnt</name>
       <description>Indicates the total number of USB packets that constitute the Transfer Size amount of data for endpoint 0.This field is decremented every time a packet (maximum size or short packet) is read from the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>19</lsb>
       <msb>28</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>rxdpid</name>
       <description>Applies to isochronous OUT endpoints only.This is the data PID received in the last packet for this endpoint. Use datax.
Applies to control OUT Endpoints only. Use packetx. This field specifies the number of back-to-back SETUP data packets the endpoint can receive.</description>
       <lsb>29</lsb>
       <msb>30</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doeptsiz12_rxdpid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data0</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data2packet1</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data1packet2</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>mdatapacket3</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdma12</name>
     <description>DMA OUT Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc94</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdma12</name>
       <description>Holds the start address of the external memory for storing or fetching endpoint data. for control endpoints, this field stores control OUT data packets as well as SETUP transaction data packets. When more than three SETUP packets are received back-to-back, the SETUP data packet in the memory is overwritten. This register is incremented on every AHB transaction. The application can give only a DWORD-aligned address.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is not enabled, the application programs the start address value in this field.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field indicates the base pointer for the descriptor list.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdmab12</name>
     <description>DMA Buffer Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc9c</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdmab12</name>
       <description>Holds the current buffer address. This register is updated as and when the data transfer for the corresponding end point is in progress. This register is present only in Scatter/Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepctl13</name>
     <description>Out Endpoint 13.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xca0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>mps</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. The application must program this field with the maximum packet size for the current logical endpoint. This value is in bytes.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>10</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>usbactep</name>
       <description>Indicates whether this endpoint is active in the current configuration and interface. The core clears this bit for all endpoints (other than EP 0) after detecting a USB reset. After receiving the SetConfiguration and SetInterface commands, the application must program endpoint registers accordingly and set this bit.</description>
       <lsb>15</lsb>
       <msb>15</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl13_usbactep_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>dpid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Contains the PID of the packet to be received or transmitted on this endpoint. The application must program the PID of the first packet to be received or transmitted on this endpoint, after the endpoint is activated. The applications use the SetD1PID and SetD0PID fields of this register to program either DATA0 or DATA1 PID.
0: DATA0
1: DATA1This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode. Even/Odd (Micro)Frame (EO_FrNum) In non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode:
Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Indicates the (micro)frame number in which the core transmits/receives isochronous data for this endpoint. The application must program the even/odd (micro) frame number in which it intends to transmit/receive isochronous data for this endpoint using the SetEvnFr and SetOddFr fields in this register.
0: Even (micro)frame
1: Odd (micro)frame
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field is reserved. The frame number in which to send data is provided in the transmit descriptor structure. The frame in which data is received is updated in receive descriptor structure.</description>
       <lsb>16</lsb>
       <msb>16</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl13_dpid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>naksts</name>
       <description>When either the application or the core sets this bit:
-The core stops receiving any data on an OUT endpoint, even if there is space in the RxFIFO to accommodate the incoming packet.
-for non-isochronous IN endpoints: The core stops transmitting any data on an IN endpoint, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
-for isochronous IN endpoints: The core sends out a zero-length data packet, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
       <lsb>17</lsb>
       <msb>17</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl13_naksts_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>nonnak</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>nak</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>eptype</name>
       <description>This is the transfer type supported by this logical endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>18</lsb>
       <msb>19</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl13_eptype_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>control</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>isochronous</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>bulk</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>interrup</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>snp</name>
       <description>Applies to OUT endpoints only. This bit configures the endpoint to Snoop mode. In Snoop mode, the core does not check the correctness of OUT packets before transferring them to application memory.</description>
       <lsb>20</lsb>
       <msb>20</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl13_snp_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disable</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enable</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>stall</name>
       <description>Applies to non-control, non-isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. The application sets this bit to stall all tokens from the USB host to this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Only the application can clear this bit, never the core. Applies to control endpoints only. The application can only set this bit, and the core clears it, when a SETUP token is received for this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
       <lsb>21</lsb>
       <msb>21</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl13_stall_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>cnak</name>
       <description>A write to this bit clears the NAK bit for the endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>26</lsb>
       <msb>26</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl13_cnak_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>snak</name>
       <description>A write to this bit sets the NAK bit for the endpoint. Using this bit, the application can control the transmission of NAK handshakes on an endpoint. The core can also Set this bit for an endpoint after a SETUP packet is received on that endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>27</lsb>
       <msb>27</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl13_snak_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setd0pid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA0. This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode. In non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode: Set Even (micro)frame (SetEvenFr) Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Even/Odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to even (micro) frame. When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field is reserved. The frame number in which to send data is in the transmit descriptor structure. The frame in which to receive data is updated in receive descriptor structure.</description>
       <lsb>28</lsb>
       <msb>28</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl13_setd0pid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setd1pid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA1. This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode.
Set Odd (micro)frame (SetOddFr) Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Even/Odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to odd (micro)frame.This field is not applicable for Scatter/Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>29</lsb>
       <msb>29</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl13_setd1pid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epdis</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. The application sets this bit to stop transmitting/receiving data on an endpoint, even before the transfer for that endpoint is complete. The application must wait for the Endpoint Disabled interrupt before treating the endpoint as disabled. The core clears this bit before setting the Endpoint Disabled interrupt. The application must set this bit only if Endpoint Enable is already set for this endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>30</lsb>
       <msb>30</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl13_epdis_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epena</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.
-When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled,
-for IN endpoints this bit indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer with data ready to transmit is setup.
-for OUT endpoint it indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer to receive data is setup.
-When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled such as for buffer-pointer based DMA mode:
- for IN endpoints, this bit indicates that data is ready to be transmitted on the endpoint.
- for OUT endpoints, this bit indicates that the application has allocated the memory to start receiving data from the USB.
- The core clears this bit before setting any of the following interrupts on this endpoint:
-SETUP Phase Done
-Endpoint Disabled
-Transfer Completed
for control endpoints in DMA mode, this bit must be set to be able to transfer SETUP data packets in memory.</description>
       <lsb>31</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl13_epena_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepint13</name>
     <description>This register indicates the status of an endpoint with respect to USB- and AHB-related events. The application must read this register when the OUT Endpoints Interrupt bit or IN Endpoints Interrupt bit of the Core Interrupt register (GINTSTS.OEPInt or GINTSTS.IEPInt, respectively) is set. Before the application can read this register, it must first read the Device All Endpoints Interrupt (DAINT) register to get the exact endpoint number for the Device Endpoint-n Interrupt register. The application must clear the appropriate bit in this register to clear the corresponding bits in the DAINT and GINTSTS registers.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xca8</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>xfercompl</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled
This field indicates that the requested data from the internal FIFO is moved to external system memory. This interrupt is generated only when the corresponding endpoint descriptor is closed, and the IOC bit for the corresponding descriptor is Set.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is disabled, this field indicates that the programmed transfer is complete on the AHB as well as on the USB, for this endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint13_xfercompl_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epdisbld</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. This bit indicates that the endpoint is disabled per the application's request.</description>
       <lsb>1</lsb>
       <msb>1</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint13_epdisbld_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>ahberr</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.This is generated only in Internal DMA mode when there is an AHB error during an AHB read/write. The application can read the corresponding endpoint DMA address register to get the error address.</description>
       <lsb>2</lsb>
       <msb>2</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint13_ahberr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setup</name>
       <description>Applies to control OUT endpoints only. Indicates that the SETUP phase for the control endpoint is complete and no more back-to-back SETUP packets were received for the current control transfer. On this interrupt, the application can decode the received SETUP data packet.</description>
       <lsb>3</lsb>
       <msb>3</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint13_setup_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>outtknepdis</name>
       <description>Applies only to control OUT endpoints. Indicates that an OUT token was received when the endpoint was not yet enabled. This interrupt is asserted on the endpoint for which the OUT token was received.</description>
       <lsb>4</lsb>
       <msb>4</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint13_outtknepdis_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>stsphsercvd</name>
       <description>This interrupt is valid only for Control OUT endpoints and only in Scatter Gather DMA mode. This interrupt is generated only after the core has transferred all the data that the host has sent during the data phase of a control write transfer, to the system memory buffer. The interrupt indicates to the application that the host has switched from data phase to the status phase of a Control Write transfer. The application can use this interrupt to ACK or STALL the Status phase, after it has decoded the data phase. This is applicable only in Case of Scatter Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>5</lsb>
       <msb>5</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint13_stsphsercvd_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>back2backsetup</name>
       <description>Applies to Control OUT endpoints only. This bit indicates that the core has received more than three back-to-back SETUP packets for this particular endpoint. for information about handling this interrupt,</description>
       <lsb>6</lsb>
       <msb>6</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint13_back2backsetup_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>outpkterr</name>
       <description>Applies to OUT endpoints Only This interrupt is asserted when the core detects an overflow or a CRC error for non-Isochronous OUT packet.</description>
       <lsb>8</lsb>
       <msb>8</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint13_outpkterr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bnaintr</name>
       <description>This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled. The core generates this interrupt when the descriptor accessed is not ready for the Core to process, such as Host busy or DMA done</description>
       <lsb>9</lsb>
       <msb>9</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint13_bnaintr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>pktdrpsts</name>
       <description>This bit indicates to the application that an ISOC OUT packet has been dropped. This bit does not have an associated mask bit and does not generate an interrupt.</description>
       <lsb>11</lsb>
       <msb>11</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint13_pktdrpsts_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bbleerr</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when babble is received for the endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>12</lsb>
       <msb>12</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint13_bbleerr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>nakintrpt</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when a NAK is transmitted or received by the device. In case of isochronous IN endpoints the interrupt gets generated when a zero length packet is transmitted due to un-availability of data in the TXFifo.</description>
       <lsb>13</lsb>
       <msb>13</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint13_nakintrpt_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>nyetintrpt</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when a NYET response is transmitted for a non isochronous OUT endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>14</lsb>
       <msb>14</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint13_nyetintrpt_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doeptsiz13</name>
     <description>The application must modify this register before enabling the endpoint. Once the endpoint is enabled using Endpoint Enable bit of the Device Endpoint-n Control registers (DIEPCTLn.EPEna/DOEPCTLn.EPEna), the core modifies this register. The application can only read this register once the core has cleared the Endpoint Enable bit.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xcb0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>xfersize</name>
       <description>Indicates the transfer size in bytes for endpoint 0. The core interrupts the application only after it has exhausted the transfer size amount of data. The transfer size can be Set to the maximum packet size of the endpoint, to be interrupted at the end of each packet. The core decrements this field every time a packet from the external memory is written to the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>18</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>pktcnt</name>
       <description>Indicates the total number of USB packets that constitute the Transfer Size amount of data for endpoint 0.This field is decremented every time a packet (maximum size or short packet) is read from the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>19</lsb>
       <msb>28</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>rxdpid</name>
       <description>Applies to isochronous OUT endpoints only.This is the data PID received in the last packet for this endpoint. Use datax.
Applies to control OUT Endpoints only. Use packetx. This field specifies the number of back-to-back SETUP data packets the endpoint can receive.</description>
       <lsb>29</lsb>
       <msb>30</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doeptsiz13_rxdpid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data0</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data2packet1</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data1packet2</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>mdatapacket3</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdma13</name>
     <description>DMA OUT Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xcb4</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdma13</name>
       <description>Holds the start address of the external memory for storing or fetching endpoint data. for control endpoints, this field stores control OUT data packets as well as SETUP transaction data packets. When more than three SETUP packets are received back-to-back, the SETUP data packet in the memory is overwritten. This register is incremented on every AHB transaction. The application can give only a DWORD-aligned address.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is not enabled, the application programs the start address value in this field.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field indicates the base pointer for the descriptor list.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdmab13</name>
     <description>DMA Buffer Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xcbc</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdmab13</name>
       <description>Holds the current buffer address. This register is updated as and when the data transfer for the corresponding end point is in progress. This register is present only in Scatter/Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepctl14</name>
     <description>Out Endpoint 14.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xcc0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>mps</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. The application must program this field with the maximum packet size for the current logical endpoint. This value is in bytes.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>10</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>usbactep</name>
       <description>Indicates whether this endpoint is active in the current configuration and interface. The core clears this bit for all endpoints (other than EP 0) after detecting a USB reset. After receiving the SetConfiguration and SetInterface commands, the application must program endpoint registers accordingly and set this bit.</description>
       <lsb>15</lsb>
       <msb>15</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl14_usbactep_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>dpid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Contains the PID of the packet to be received or transmitted on this endpoint. The application must program the PID of the first packet to be received or transmitted on this endpoint, after the endpoint is activated. The applications use the SetD1PID and SetD0PID fields of this register to program either DATA0 or DATA1 PID.
0: DATA0
1: DATA1This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode. Even/Odd (Micro)Frame (EO_FrNum) In non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode:
Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Indicates the (micro)frame number in which the core transmits/receives isochronous data for this endpoint. The application must program the even/odd (micro) frame number in which it intends to transmit/receive isochronous data for this endpoint using the SetEvnFr and SetOddFr fields in this register.
0: Even (micro)frame
1: Odd (micro)frame
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field is reserved. The frame number in which to send data is provided in the transmit descriptor structure. The frame in which data is received is updated in receive descriptor structure.</description>
       <lsb>16</lsb>
       <msb>16</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl14_dpid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>naksts</name>
       <description>When either the application or the core sets this bit:
-The core stops receiving any data on an OUT endpoint, even if there is space in the RxFIFO to accommodate the incoming packet.
-for non-isochronous IN endpoints: The core stops transmitting any data on an IN endpoint, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
-for isochronous IN endpoints: The core sends out a zero-length data packet, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
       <lsb>17</lsb>
       <msb>17</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl14_naksts_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>nonnak</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>nak</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>eptype</name>
       <description>This is the transfer type supported by this logical endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>18</lsb>
       <msb>19</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl14_eptype_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>control</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>isochronous</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>bulk</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>interrup</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>snp</name>
       <description>Applies to OUT endpoints only. This bit configures the endpoint to Snoop mode. In Snoop mode, the core does not check the correctness of OUT packets before transferring them to application memory.</description>
       <lsb>20</lsb>
       <msb>20</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl14_snp_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disable</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enable</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>stall</name>
       <description>Applies to non-control, non-isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. The application sets this bit to stall all tokens from the USB host to this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Only the application can clear this bit, never the core. Applies to control endpoints only. The application can only set this bit, and the core clears it, when a SETUP token is received for this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
       <lsb>21</lsb>
       <msb>21</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl14_stall_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>cnak</name>
       <description>A write to this bit clears the NAK bit for the endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>26</lsb>
       <msb>26</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl14_cnak_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>snak</name>
       <description>A write to this bit sets the NAK bit for the endpoint. Using this bit, the application can control the transmission of NAK handshakes on an endpoint. The core can also Set this bit for an endpoint after a SETUP packet is received on that endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>27</lsb>
       <msb>27</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl14_snak_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setd0pid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA0. This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode. In non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode: Set Even (micro)frame (SetEvenFr) Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Even/Odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to even (micro) frame. When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field is reserved. The frame number in which to send data is in the transmit descriptor structure. The frame in which to receive data is updated in receive descriptor structure.</description>
       <lsb>28</lsb>
       <msb>28</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl14_setd0pid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setd1pid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA1. This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode.
Set Odd (micro)frame (SetOddFr) Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Even/Odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to odd (micro)frame.This field is not applicable for Scatter/Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>29</lsb>
       <msb>29</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl14_setd1pid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epdis</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. The application sets this bit to stop transmitting/receiving data on an endpoint, even before the transfer for that endpoint is complete. The application must wait for the Endpoint Disabled interrupt before treating the endpoint as disabled. The core clears this bit before setting the Endpoint Disabled interrupt. The application must set this bit only if Endpoint Enable is already set for this endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>30</lsb>
       <msb>30</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl14_epdis_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epena</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.
-When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled,
-for IN endpoints this bit indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer with data ready to transmit is setup.
-for OUT endpoint it indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer to receive data is setup.
-When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled such as for buffer-pointer based DMA mode:
- for IN endpoints, this bit indicates that data is ready to be transmitted on the endpoint.
- for OUT endpoints, this bit indicates that the application has allocated the memory to start receiving data from the USB.
- The core clears this bit before setting any of the following interrupts on this endpoint:
-SETUP Phase Done
-Endpoint Disabled
-Transfer Completed
for control endpoints in DMA mode, this bit must be set to be able to transfer SETUP data packets in memory.</description>
       <lsb>31</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl14_epena_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepint14</name>
     <description>This register indicates the status of an endpoint with respect to USB- and AHB-related events. The application must read this register when the OUT Endpoints Interrupt bit or IN Endpoints Interrupt bit of the Core Interrupt register (GINTSTS.OEPInt or GINTSTS.IEPInt, respectively) is set. Before the application can read this register, it must first read the Device All Endpoints Interrupt (DAINT) register to get the exact endpoint number for the Device Endpoint-n Interrupt register. The application must clear the appropriate bit in this register to clear the corresponding bits in the DAINT and GINTSTS registers.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xcc8</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>xfercompl</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled
This field indicates that the requested data from the internal FIFO is moved to external system memory. This interrupt is generated only when the corresponding endpoint descriptor is closed, and the IOC bit for the corresponding descriptor is Set.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is disabled, this field indicates that the programmed transfer is complete on the AHB as well as on the USB, for this endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint14_xfercompl_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epdisbld</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. This bit indicates that the endpoint is disabled per the application's request.</description>
       <lsb>1</lsb>
       <msb>1</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint14_epdisbld_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>ahberr</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.This is generated only in Internal DMA mode when there is an AHB error during an AHB read/write. The application can read the corresponding endpoint DMA address register to get the error address.</description>
       <lsb>2</lsb>
       <msb>2</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint14_ahberr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setup</name>
       <description>Applies to control OUT endpoints only. Indicates that the SETUP phase for the control endpoint is complete and no more back-to-back SETUP packets were received for the current control transfer. On this interrupt, the application can decode the received SETUP data packet.</description>
       <lsb>3</lsb>
       <msb>3</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint14_setup_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>outtknepdis</name>
       <description>Applies only to control OUT endpoints. Indicates that an OUT token was received when the endpoint was not yet enabled. This interrupt is asserted on the endpoint for which the OUT token was received.</description>
       <lsb>4</lsb>
       <msb>4</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint14_outtknepdis_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>stsphsercvd</name>
       <description>This interrupt is valid only for Control OUT endpoints and only in Scatter Gather DMA mode. This interrupt is generated only after the core has transferred all the data that the host has sent during the data phase of a control write transfer, to the system memory buffer. The interrupt indicates to the application that the host has switched from data phase to the status phase of a Control Write transfer. The application can use this interrupt to ACK or STALL the Status phase, after it has decoded the data phase. This is applicable only in Case of Scatter Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>5</lsb>
       <msb>5</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint14_stsphsercvd_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>back2backsetup</name>
       <description>Applies to Control OUT endpoints only. This bit indicates that the core has received more than three back-to-back SETUP packets for this particular endpoint. for information about handling this interrupt,</description>
       <lsb>6</lsb>
       <msb>6</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint14_back2backsetup_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>outpkterr</name>
       <description>Applies to OUT endpoints Only This interrupt is asserted when the core detects an overflow or a CRC error for non-Isochronous OUT packet.</description>
       <lsb>8</lsb>
       <msb>8</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint14_outpkterr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bnaintr</name>
       <description>This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled. The core generates this interrupt when the descriptor accessed is not ready for the Core to process, such as Host busy or DMA done</description>
       <lsb>9</lsb>
       <msb>9</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint14_bnaintr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>pktdrpsts</name>
       <description>This bit indicates to the application that an ISOC OUT packet has been dropped. This bit does not have an associated mask bit and does not generate an interrupt.</description>
       <lsb>11</lsb>
       <msb>11</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint14_pktdrpsts_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bbleerr</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when babble is received for the endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>12</lsb>
       <msb>12</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint14_bbleerr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>nakintrpt</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when a NAK is transmitted or received by the device. In case of isochronous IN endpoints the interrupt gets generated when a zero length packet is transmitted due to un-availability of data in the TXFifo.</description>
       <lsb>13</lsb>
       <msb>13</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint14_nakintrpt_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>nyetintrpt</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when a NYET response is transmitted for a non isochronous OUT endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>14</lsb>
       <msb>14</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint14_nyetintrpt_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doeptsiz14</name>
     <description>The application must modify this register before enabling the endpoint. Once the endpoint is enabled using Endpoint Enable bit of the Device Endpoint-n Control registers (DIEPCTLn.EPEna/DOEPCTLn.EPEna), the core modifies this register. The application can only read this register once the core has cleared the Endpoint Enable bit.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xcd0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>xfersize</name>
       <description>Indicates the transfer size in bytes for endpoint 0. The core interrupts the application only after it has exhausted the transfer size amount of data. The transfer size can be Set to the maximum packet size of the endpoint, to be interrupted at the end of each packet. The core decrements this field every time a packet from the external memory is written to the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>18</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>pktcnt</name>
       <description>Indicates the total number of USB packets that constitute the Transfer Size amount of data for endpoint 0.This field is decremented every time a packet (maximum size or short packet) is read from the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>19</lsb>
       <msb>28</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>rxdpid</name>
       <description>Applies to isochronous OUT endpoints only.This is the data PID received in the last packet for this endpoint. Use datax.
Applies to control OUT Endpoints only. Use packetx. This field specifies the number of back-to-back SETUP data packets the endpoint can receive.</description>
       <lsb>29</lsb>
       <msb>30</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doeptsiz14_rxdpid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data0</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data2packet1</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data1packet2</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>mdatapacket3</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdma14</name>
     <description>DMA OUT Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xcd4</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdma14</name>
       <description>Holds the start address of the external memory for storing or fetching endpoint data. for control endpoints, this field stores control OUT data packets as well as SETUP transaction data packets. When more than three SETUP packets are received back-to-back, the SETUP data packet in the memory is overwritten. This register is incremented on every AHB transaction. The application can give only a DWORD-aligned address.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is not enabled, the application programs the start address value in this field.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field indicates the base pointer for the descriptor list.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdmab14</name>
     <description>DMA Buffer Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xcdc</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdmab14</name>
       <description>Holds the current buffer address. This register is updated as and when the data transfer for the corresponding end point is in progress. This register is present only in Scatter/Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepctl15</name>
     <description>Out Endpoint 15.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xce0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>mps</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. The application must program this field with the maximum packet size for the current logical endpoint. This value is in bytes.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>10</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>usbactep</name>
       <description>Indicates whether this endpoint is active in the current configuration and interface. The core clears this bit for all endpoints (other than EP 0) after detecting a USB reset. After receiving the SetConfiguration and SetInterface commands, the application must program endpoint registers accordingly and set this bit.</description>
       <lsb>15</lsb>
       <msb>15</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl15_usbactep_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>dpid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Contains the PID of the packet to be received or transmitted on this endpoint. The application must program the PID of the first packet to be received or transmitted on this endpoint, after the endpoint is activated. The applications use the SetD1PID and SetD0PID fields of this register to program either DATA0 or DATA1 PID.
0: DATA0
1: DATA1This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode. Even/Odd (Micro)Frame (EO_FrNum) In non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode:
Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Indicates the (micro)frame number in which the core transmits/receives isochronous data for this endpoint. The application must program the even/odd (micro) frame number in which it intends to transmit/receive isochronous data for this endpoint using the SetEvnFr and SetOddFr fields in this register.
0: Even (micro)frame
1: Odd (micro)frame
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field is reserved. The frame number in which to send data is provided in the transmit descriptor structure. The frame in which data is received is updated in receive descriptor structure.</description>
       <lsb>16</lsb>
       <msb>16</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl15_dpid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>naksts</name>
       <description>When either the application or the core sets this bit:
-The core stops receiving any data on an OUT endpoint, even if there is space in the RxFIFO to accommodate the incoming packet.
-for non-isochronous IN endpoints: The core stops transmitting any data on an IN endpoint, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
-for isochronous IN endpoints: The core sends out a zero-length data packet, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
       <lsb>17</lsb>
       <msb>17</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl15_naksts_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>nonnak</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>nak</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>eptype</name>
       <description>This is the transfer type supported by this logical endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>18</lsb>
       <msb>19</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl15_eptype_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>control</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>isochronous</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>bulk</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>interrup</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>snp</name>
       <description>Applies to OUT endpoints only. This bit configures the endpoint to Snoop mode. In Snoop mode, the core does not check the correctness of OUT packets before transferring them to application memory.</description>
       <lsb>20</lsb>
       <msb>20</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl15_snp_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disable</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enable</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>stall</name>
       <description>Applies to non-control, non-isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. The application sets this bit to stall all tokens from the USB host to this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Only the application can clear this bit, never the core. Applies to control endpoints only. The application can only set this bit, and the core clears it, when a SETUP token is received for this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
       <lsb>21</lsb>
       <msb>21</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl15_stall_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>cnak</name>
       <description>A write to this bit clears the NAK bit for the endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>26</lsb>
       <msb>26</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl15_cnak_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>snak</name>
       <description>A write to this bit sets the NAK bit for the endpoint. Using this bit, the application can control the transmission of NAK handshakes on an endpoint. The core can also Set this bit for an endpoint after a SETUP packet is received on that endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>27</lsb>
       <msb>27</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl15_snak_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setd0pid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA0. This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode. In non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode: Set Even (micro)frame (SetEvenFr) Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Even/Odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to even (micro) frame. When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field is reserved. The frame number in which to send data is in the transmit descriptor structure. The frame in which to receive data is updated in receive descriptor structure.</description>
       <lsb>28</lsb>
       <msb>28</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl15_setd0pid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setd1pid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA1. This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode.
Set Odd (micro)frame (SetOddFr) Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Even/Odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to odd (micro)frame.This field is not applicable for Scatter/Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>29</lsb>
       <msb>29</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl15_setd1pid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epdis</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. The application sets this bit to stop transmitting/receiving data on an endpoint, even before the transfer for that endpoint is complete. The application must wait for the Endpoint Disabled interrupt before treating the endpoint as disabled. The core clears this bit before setting the Endpoint Disabled interrupt. The application must set this bit only if Endpoint Enable is already set for this endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>30</lsb>
       <msb>30</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl15_epdis_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epena</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.
-When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled,
-for IN endpoints this bit indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer with data ready to transmit is setup.
-for OUT endpoint it indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer to receive data is setup.
-When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled such as for buffer-pointer based DMA mode:
- for IN endpoints, this bit indicates that data is ready to be transmitted on the endpoint.
- for OUT endpoints, this bit indicates that the application has allocated the memory to start receiving data from the USB.
- The core clears this bit before setting any of the following interrupts on this endpoint:
-SETUP Phase Done
-Endpoint Disabled
-Transfer Completed
for control endpoints in DMA mode, this bit must be set to be able to transfer SETUP data packets in memory.</description>
       <lsb>31</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl15_epena_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepint15</name>
     <description>This register indicates the status of an endpoint with respect to USB- and AHB-related events. The application must read this register when the OUT Endpoints Interrupt bit or IN Endpoints Interrupt bit of the Core Interrupt register (GINTSTS.OEPInt or GINTSTS.IEPInt, respectively) is set. Before the application can read this register, it must first read the Device All Endpoints Interrupt (DAINT) register to get the exact endpoint number for the Device Endpoint-n Interrupt register. The application must clear the appropriate bit in this register to clear the corresponding bits in the DAINT and GINTSTS registers.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xce8</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>xfercompl</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled
This field indicates that the requested data from the internal FIFO is moved to external system memory. This interrupt is generated only when the corresponding endpoint descriptor is closed, and the IOC bit for the corresponding descriptor is Set.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is disabled, this field indicates that the programmed transfer is complete on the AHB as well as on the USB, for this endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint15_xfercompl_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epdisbld</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. This bit indicates that the endpoint is disabled per the application's request.</description>
       <lsb>1</lsb>
       <msb>1</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint15_epdisbld_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>ahberr</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.This is generated only in Internal DMA mode when there is an AHB error during an AHB read/write. The application can read the corresponding endpoint DMA address register to get the error address.</description>
       <lsb>2</lsb>
       <msb>2</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint15_ahberr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setup</name>
       <description>Applies to control OUT endpoints only. Indicates that the SETUP phase for the control endpoint is complete and no more back-to-back SETUP packets were received for the current control transfer. On this interrupt, the application can decode the received SETUP data packet.</description>
       <lsb>3</lsb>
       <msb>3</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint15_setup_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>outtknepdis</name>
       <description>Applies only to control OUT endpoints. Indicates that an OUT token was received when the endpoint was not yet enabled. This interrupt is asserted on the endpoint for which the OUT token was received.</description>
       <lsb>4</lsb>
       <msb>4</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint15_outtknepdis_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>stsphsercvd</name>
       <description>This interrupt is valid only for Control OUT endpoints and only in Scatter Gather DMA mode. This interrupt is generated only after the core has transferred all the data that the host has sent during the data phase of a control write transfer, to the system memory buffer. The interrupt indicates to the application that the host has switched from data phase to the status phase of a Control Write transfer. The application can use this interrupt to ACK or STALL the Status phase, after it has decoded the data phase. This is applicable only in Case of Scatter Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>5</lsb>
       <msb>5</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint15_stsphsercvd_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>back2backsetup</name>
       <description>Applies to Control OUT endpoints only. This bit indicates that the core has received more than three back-to-back SETUP packets for this particular endpoint. for information about handling this interrupt,</description>
       <lsb>6</lsb>
       <msb>6</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint15_back2backsetup_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>outpkterr</name>
       <description>Applies to OUT endpoints Only This interrupt is asserted when the core detects an overflow or a CRC error for non-Isochronous OUT packet.</description>
       <lsb>8</lsb>
       <msb>8</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint15_outpkterr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bnaintr</name>
       <description>This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled. The core generates this interrupt when the descriptor accessed is not ready for the Core to process, such as Host busy or DMA done</description>
       <lsb>9</lsb>
       <msb>9</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint15_bnaintr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>pktdrpsts</name>
       <description>This bit indicates to the application that an ISOC OUT packet has been dropped. This bit does not have an associated mask bit and does not generate an interrupt.</description>
       <lsb>11</lsb>
       <msb>11</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint15_pktdrpsts_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bbleerr</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when babble is received for the endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>12</lsb>
       <msb>12</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint15_bbleerr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>nakintrpt</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when a NAK is transmitted or received by the device. In case of isochronous IN endpoints the interrupt gets generated when a zero length packet is transmitted due to un-availability of data in the TXFifo.</description>
       <lsb>13</lsb>
       <msb>13</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint15_nakintrpt_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>nyetintrpt</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when a NYET response is transmitted for a non isochronous OUT endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>14</lsb>
       <msb>14</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint15_nyetintrpt_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doeptsiz15</name>
     <description>The application must modify this register before enabling the endpoint. Once the endpoint is enabled using Endpoint Enable bit of the Device Endpoint-n Control registers (DIEPCTLn.EPEna/DOEPCTLn.EPEna), the core modifies this register. The application can only read this register once the core has cleared the Endpoint Enable bit.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xcf0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>xfersize</name>
       <description>Indicates the transfer size in bytes for endpoint 0. The core interrupts the application only after it has exhausted the transfer size amount of data. The transfer size can be Set to the maximum packet size of the endpoint, to be interrupted at the end of each packet. The core decrements this field every time a packet from the external memory is written to the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>18</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>pktcnt</name>
       <description>Indicates the total number of USB packets that constitute the Transfer Size amount of data for endpoint 0.This field is decremented every time a packet (maximum size or short packet) is read from the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>19</lsb>
       <msb>28</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>rxdpid</name>
       <description>Applies to isochronous OUT endpoints only.This is the data PID received in the last packet for this endpoint. Use datax.
Applies to control OUT Endpoints only. Use packetx. This field specifies the number of back-to-back SETUP data packets the endpoint can receive.</description>
       <lsb>29</lsb>
       <msb>30</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doeptsiz15_rxdpid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data0</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data2packet1</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data1packet2</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>mdatapacket3</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdma15</name>
     <description>DMA OUT Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xcf4</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdma15</name>
       <description>Holds the start address of the external memory for storing or fetching endpoint data. for control endpoints, this field stores control OUT data packets as well as SETUP transaction data packets. When more than three SETUP packets are received back-to-back, the SETUP data packet in the memory is overwritten. This register is incremented on every AHB transaction. The application can give only a DWORD-aligned address.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is not enabled, the application programs the start address value in this field.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field indicates the base pointer for the descriptor list.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdmab15</name>
     <description>DMA Buffer Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xcfc</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdmab15</name>
       <description>Holds the current buffer address. This register is updated as and when the data transfer for the corresponding end point is in progress. This register is present only in Scatter/Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>pwrclkgrp_pcgcctl</name>
     <description>This register is available in Host and Device modes. The application can use this register to control the core's power-down and clock gating features. Because the CSR module is turned off during power-down, this register is implemented in the AHB Slave BIU module.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xe00</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>stoppclk</name>
       <description>The application sets this bit to stop the PHY clock (phy_clk) when the USB is suspended, the session is not valid, or the device is disconnected. The application clears this bit when the USB is resumed or a new session starts.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_pwrclkgrp_pcgcctl_stoppclk_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>rstpdwnmodule</name>
       <description>This bit is valid only in Partial Power-Down mode. Theapplication sets this bit when the power is turned off. The application clears this bit after the power is turned on and the PHY clock is up. The R/W of all core registers are possible only when this bit is set to 1b0.</description>
       <lsb>3</lsb>
       <msb>3</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_pwrclkgrp_pcgcctl_rstpdwnmodule_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>on</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>off</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>physleep</name>
       <description>Indicates that the PHY is in Sleep State.</description>
       <lsb>6</lsb>
       <msb>6</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_pwrclkgrp_pcgcctl_physleep_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>l1suspended</name>
       <description>Indicates that the PHY is in deep sleep when in L1 state.</description>
       <lsb>7</lsb>
       <msb>7</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_pwrclkgrp_pcgcctl_l1suspended_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
   </registers>
  </peripheral>
  <peripheral>
   <name>usb1</name>
   <version>0.0</version>
   <description>Registers in the USB OTG Controller Module.
Only the Core Global, Power and Clock Gating, Data FIFO Access, and Host Port registers can be accessedin both Host and Device modes. When the USB OTG Controller is operating in one mode, either Device or Host, the application must not access registers from the other mode. If an illegal access occurs, a Mode Mismatch interrupt is generated and reflected in the Core Interrupt register (GINTSTS.ModeMis).
When the core switches from one mode to another, the registers in the new mode must be reprogrammed as they would be after a power-on reset.
The register address map is fixed and does not depend on the module configuration (for example, how many endpoints are implemented). Host and Device mode registers occupy different addresses.</description>
   <disableCondition>(Peripherals-&gt;rstmgr-&gt;rstmgr_permodrst-&gt;usb1 == 1)</disableCondition><baseAddress>0xffb40000</baseAddress>
   <access>read-write</access>
   <addressBlock>
    <offset>0x0</offset>
    <size>0x40000</size>
    <usage>registers</usage>
   </addressBlock>
   <registers>
    <register>
     <name>globgrp_gotgctl</name>
     <description>The OTG Control and Status register controls the behavior and reflects the status of the OTG function.</description>
     <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x10000</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>sesreqscs</name>
       <description>This bit is set when a session request initiation is successful. This bit is valid only For Device Only configuration when OTG_MODE == 3 or OTG_MODE == 4. Applies for device only.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gotgctl_sesreqscs_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>fail</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>success</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>sesreq</name>
       <description>The application sets this bit to initiate a session request on the USB. The application can clear this bit by writing a 0 when the Host Negotiation Success Status Change bit in the OTG Interrupt register (GOTGINT.HstNegSucStsChng) is SET. The core clears this bit when the HstNegSucStsChng bit is cleared. If you use the USB 1.1 Full-Speed Serial Transceiver interface to initiate the session request, the application must wait until the VBUS discharges to 0.2 V, after the B-Session Valid bit in  this register (GOTGCTL.BSesVld) is cleared. This discharge time varies between different PHYs and can be obtained from the PHY vendor.
</description>
       <lsb>1</lsb>
       <msb>1</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gotgctl_sesreq_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>norequest</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>request</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>vbvalidoven</name>
       <description>This bit is used to enable/disable the software to override the vbus-valid signal using the GOTGCTL.vbvalidOvVal..</description>
       <lsb>2</lsb>
       <msb>2</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gotgctl_vbvalidoven_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>vbvalidovval</name>
       <description>This bit is used to set Override value for vbus valid signal when GOTGCTL.VbvalidOvEn is set.</description>
       <lsb>3</lsb>
       <msb>3</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gotgctl_vbvalidovval_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>set0</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>set1</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>avalidoven</name>
       <description>This bit is used to enable/disable the software to override the Avalid signal using the GOTGCTL.AvalidOvVal.</description>
       <lsb>4</lsb>
       <msb>4</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gotgctl_avalidoven_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>avalidovval</name>
       <description>This bit is used to set Override value for Avalid signal when GOTGCTL.BvalidOvEn is set.</description>
       <lsb>5</lsb>
       <msb>5</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gotgctl_avalidovval_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>value0</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>value1</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bvalidoven</name>
       <description>This bit is used to enable/disable the software to  override the Bvalid signal using the GOTGCTL.BvalidOvVal.</description>
       <lsb>6</lsb>
       <msb>6</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gotgctl_bvalidoven_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bvalidovval</name>
       <description>This bit is used to set Override value for Bvalid signalwhen GOTGCTL.BvalidOvEn is set.</description>
       <lsb>7</lsb>
       <msb>7</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gotgctl_bvalidovval_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>value0</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>value1</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>hstnegscs</name>
       <description>Mode: Device only. Host Negotiation Success (HstNegScs) The core sets this bit when host negotiation is successful. The core clears this bit when the HNP Request (HNPReq) bit in this register is SET.</description>
       <lsb>8</lsb>
       <msb>8</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gotgctl_hstnegscs_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>fail</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>success</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>hnpreq</name>
       <description>Mode: Device only. The application sets this bit to initiate an HNP request to the connected USB host. The application can clear this bit by writing a 0 when the Host Negotiation Success Status Change bit in the OTG Interrupt register (GOTGINT.HstNegSucStsChng) is SET.The core clears this bit when the HstNegSucStsChng bit iscleared. </description>
       <lsb>9</lsb>
       <msb>9</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gotgctl_hnpreq_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>hstsethnpen</name>
       <description>Mode: Host only. The application sets this bit when it has successfully enabled HNP (using the SetFeature.SetHNPEnable command) on the connected device.</description>
       <lsb>10</lsb>
       <msb>10</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gotgctl_hstsethnpen_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>devhnpen</name>
       <description>Mode: Device only. The application sets this bit when it successfully receives a SetFeature.SetHNPEnable command from the connected USB host.</description>
       <lsb>11</lsb>
       <msb>11</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gotgctl_devhnpen_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>conidsts</name>
       <description>Mode: Host and Device. Indicates the connector ID status on a connect event.This bit is valid only for Host and Device mode.</description>
       <lsb>16</lsb>
       <msb>16</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gotgctl_conidsts_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>modea</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>modeb</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>dbnctime</name>
       <description>Mode: Host only. Indicates the debounce time of a detected connection.</description>
       <lsb>17</lsb>
       <msb>17</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gotgctl_dbnctime_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>long</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>short</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>asesvld</name>
       <description>Mode: Host only. Indicates the Host mode transceiver status. If you do not enabled OTG features (such as SRP and HNP), the read reset value will be 1.The vbus assigns the values internally for non-SRP or non-HNP configurations.</description>
       <lsb>18</lsb>
       <msb>18</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gotgctl_asesvld_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>valid</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>notvalid</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bsesvld</name>
       <description>Mode: Device only. Indicates the Device mode transceiver status. In OTG mode, you can use this bit to determine IF the device is connected or disconnected. If you do not enable OTG features (such as SRP and HNP), the read reset value will be 1. The vbus assigns the values internally for non-SRP or non-HNP configurations.</description>
       <lsb>19</lsb>
       <msb>19</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gotgctl_bsesvld_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>notvalid</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>valid</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>otgver</name>
       <description>Indicates the OTG revision. In OTG Version 1.3. the core supports Data line pulsing and VBus pulsing for SRP. In OTG Version 2.0 the core supports only Data line pulsing for SRP.</description>
       <lsb>20</lsb>
       <msb>20</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gotgctl_otgver_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>ver13</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>ver20</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>globgrp_gotgint</name>
     <description>The application reads this register whenever there is an OTG interrupt and clears the bits in this register to clear the OTG interrupt.</description>
     <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>sesenddet</name>
       <description>Mode:Host and Device.This bit can be set only by the core and the application should write 1 to clear it.</description>
       <lsb>2</lsb>
       <msb>2</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gotgint_sesenddet_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>sesreqsucstschng</name>
       <description>Mode: Host and Device. The core sets this bit on the success or failure of a session request. The application must read the Session Request Success bit in the OTG Control and Status register (GOTGCTL.SesReqScs) to check for success or failure. This bit can be set only by the core and the application should write 1 to clear it. </description>
       <lsb>8</lsb>
       <msb>8</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gotgint_sesreqsucstschng_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>hstnegsucstschng</name>
       <description>Mode: Host and Device. The core sets this bit on the success or failure of a USB host negotiation request. The application must read the Host Negotiation Success bit of the OTG Control and Status register (GOTGCTL.HstNegScs) to check for success or failure. This bit can be set only by the core and the application should write 1 to clear it.</description>
       <lsb>9</lsb>
       <msb>9</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gotgint_hstnegsucstschng_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>hstnegdet</name>
       <description>Mode:Host and Device. The core sets this bit when it detects a host negotiation request on the USB. This bit can be set only by the core and the application should write 1 to clear it.</description>
       <lsb>17</lsb>
       <msb>17</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gotgint_hstnegdet_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>adevtoutchg</name>
       <description>Mode:Host and Device. The core sets this bit to indicate that the A-device has timed out WHILE waiting FOR the B-device to connect. This bit can be set only by the core and the application should write 1 to clear it.</description>
       <lsb>18</lsb>
       <msb>18</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gotgint_adevtoutchg_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>dbncedone</name>
       <description>Mode: Host only. The core sets this bit when the debounce is completed after the device connect. The application can start driving USB reset after seeing this interrupt. This bit is only valid when the HNP Capable or SRP Capable bit is SET in the Core USB Configuration register (GUSBCFG.HNPCap or GUSBCFG.SRPCap, respectively). This bit can be set only by the core and the application should write 1 to clear it.</description>
       <lsb>19</lsb>
       <msb>19</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gotgint_dbncedone_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>globgrp_gahbcfg</name>
     <description>This register can be used to configure the core after power-on or a change in mode. This register mainly contains AHB system-related configuration parameters. Do not change this register after the initial programming. The application must program this register before starting any transactions on either the AHB or the USB.</description>
     <addressOffset>0x8</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>glblintrmsk</name>
       <description>Mode: Host and device. The application uses this bit to mask or unmask the interrupt line assertion to itself. Irrespective of this bits setting, the interrupt status registers are updated by the core.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gahbcfg_glblintrmsk_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>mask</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>nomask</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>hbstlen</name>
       <description>Mode:Host and device. This field is used in Internal DMA modes.</description>
       <lsb>1</lsb>
       <msb>4</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gahbcfg_hbstlen_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>word1orsingle</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>word4orincr</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>word8</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>word16orincr4</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>word32</name>
          <value>4</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>word64orincr8</name>
          <value>5</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>word128</name>
          <value>6</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>word256orincr16</name>
          <value>7</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>wordx</name>
          <value>8</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>dmaen</name>
       <description>Mode:Host and device. Enables switching from DMA mode to slave mode.</description>
       <lsb>5</lsb>
       <msb>5</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gahbcfg_dmaen_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>slavemode</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>dmamode</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>nptxfemplvl</name>
       <description>Mode:Host and device. This bit is used only in Slave mode. In host mode and with Shared FIFO with device mode, this bit indicates when the Non-Periodic TxFIFO Empty Interrupt bit in the Core Interrupt register (GINTSTS.NPTxFEmp) is triggered. With dedicated FIFO in device mode, this bit indicates when IN endpoint Transmit FIFO empty interrupt (DIEPINTn.TxFEmp) is triggered. Host mode and with Shared FIFO with device mode:
</description>
       <lsb>7</lsb>
       <msb>7</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gahbcfg_nptxfemplvl_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>halfempty</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>empty</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>ptxfemplvl</name>
       <description>Mode:Host only. Indicates when the Periodic TxFIFO Empty Interrupt bit in the Core Interrupt register (GINTSTS.PTxFEmp) is triggered. This bit is used only in Slave mode.</description>
       <lsb>8</lsb>
       <msb>8</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gahbcfg_ptxfemplvl_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>halfempty</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>empty</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>remmemsupp</name>
       <description>This bit is programmed to enable/disable the functionality to wait for the system DMA Done Signal for the DMA Write Transfers.
-The int_dma_req output signal is asserted when HSOTG DMA starts write transfer to the external memory. When the core is done with the Transfers it asserts int_dma_done signal to flag the completion of DMA writes from HSOTG. The core then waits for sys_dma_done signal from the system to proceed further and complete the Data Transfer corresponding to a particular Channel/Endpoint.
-The int_dma_req and int_dma_done signals are not asserted and the core proceeds with the assertion of the XferComp interrupt as soon as wait for the system DMA Done Signal for the DMA Write Transfers the DMA write transfer is done at the HSOTG Core Boundary and it doesn't wait for the sys_dma_done signal to complete the DATA</description>
       <lsb>21</lsb>
       <msb>21</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gahbcfg_remmemsupp_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>notialldmawrit</name>
       <description>This bit is programmed to enable the System DMA Done functionality for all the DMA write Transactions corresponding to the Channel/Endpoint. This bit is valid only when GAHBCFG.RemMemSupp is set to 1.
</description>
       <lsb>22</lsb>
       <msb>22</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gahbcfg_notialldmawrit_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>alltrans</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>lasttrans</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>globgrp_gusbcfg</name>
     <description>This register can be used to configure the core after power-on or a changing to Host mode or Device mode. It contains USB and USB-PHY related configuration parameters. The application must program this register before starting any transactions on either the AHB or the USB. Do not make changes to this register after the initial programming.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x1410</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>toutcal</name>
       <description>Mode:Host and Device. The number of PHY clocks that the application programs in this field is added to the high-speed/full-speed interpacket timeout duration in the core to account for any additional delays introduced by the PHY. This can be required, because the delay introduced by the PHY in generating the linestate condition can vary from one PHY to another. The USB standard timeout value for high-speed operation is 736 to 816 (inclusive) bit times. The USB standard timeout value for full-speed operation is 16 to 18 (inclusive) bit times. The application must program this field based on the speed of enumeration. The number of bit times added per PHY clock are: High-speed operation:
-One 30-MHz PHY clock = 16 bit times
-One 60-MHz PHY clock = 8 bit times
Full-speed operation:
-One 30-MHz PHY clock = 0.4 bit times
-One 60-MHz PHY clock = 0.2 bit times
-One 48-MHz PHY clock = 0.25 bit times</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>2</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>phyif</name>
       <description>Mode:Host and Device. This application uses a ULPI interface only. Hence only 8-bit setting is relevant. This setting should not matter since UTMI is not enabled.</description>
       <lsb>3</lsb>
       <msb>3</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_globgrp_gusbcfg_phyif_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>bits8</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>ulpi_utmi_sel</name>
       <description>Mode:Host and Device. The application uses ULPI Only in 8bit mode.
</description>
       <lsb>4</lsb>
       <msb>4</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
