    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doeptsiz8</name>
     <description>The application must modify this register before enabling the endpoint. Once the endpoint is enabled using Endpoint Enable bit of the Device Endpoint-n Control registers (DIEPCTLn.EPEna/DOEPCTLn.EPEna), the core modifies this register. The application can only read this register once the core has cleared the Endpoint Enable bit.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc10</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>xfersize</name>
       <description>Indicates the transfer size in bytes for endpoint 0. The core interrupts the application only after it has exhausted the transfer size amount of data. The transfer size can be Set to the maximum packet size of the endpoint, to be interrupted at the end of each packet. The core decrements this field every time a packet from the external memory is written to the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>18</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>pktcnt</name>
       <description>Indicates the total number of USB packets that constitute the Transfer Size amount of data for endpoint 0.This field is decremented every time a packet (maximum size or short packet) is read from the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>19</lsb>
       <msb>28</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>rxdpid</name>
       <description>Applies to isochronous OUT endpoints only.This is the data PID received in the last packet for this endpoint. Use datax.
Applies to control OUT Endpoints only. Use packetx. This field specifies the number of back-to-back SETUP data packets the endpoint can receive.</description>
       <lsb>29</lsb>
       <msb>30</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doeptsiz8_rxdpid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data0</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data2packet1</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data1packet2</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>mdatapacket3</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdma8</name>
     <description>DMA OUT Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc14</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdma8</name>
       <description>Holds the start address of the external memory for storing or fetching endpoint data. for control endpoints, this field stores control OUT data packets as well as SETUP transaction data packets. When more than three SETUP packets are received back-to-back, the SETUP data packet in the memory is overwritten. This register is incremented on every AHB transaction. The application can give only a DWORD-aligned address.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is not enabled, the application programs the start address value in this field.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field indicates the base pointer for the descriptor list.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdmab8</name>
     <description>DMA Buffer Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc1c</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdmab8</name>
       <description>Holds the current buffer address. This register is updated as and when the data transfer for the corresponding end point is in progress. This register is present only in Scatter/Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepctl9</name>
     <description>Out Endpoint 9.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc20</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>mps</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. The application must program this field with the maximum packet size for the current logical endpoint. This value is in bytes.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>10</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>usbactep</name>
       <description>Indicates whether this endpoint is active in the current configuration and interface. The core clears this bit for all endpoints (other than EP 0) after detecting a USB reset. After receiving the SetConfiguration and SetInterface commands, the application must program endpoint registers accordingly and set this bit.</description>
       <lsb>15</lsb>
       <msb>15</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl9_usbactep_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>dpid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Contains the PID of the packet to be received or transmitted on this endpoint. The application must program the PID of the first packet to be received or transmitted on this endpoint, after the endpoint is activated. The applications use the SetD1PID and SetD0PID fields of this register to program either DATA0 or DATA1 PID.
 0: DATA0
 1: DATA1This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode. Even/Odd (Micro)Frame (EO_FrNum) In non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode:
Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Indicates the (micro)frame number in which the core transmits/receives isochronous data for this endpoint. The application must program the even/odd (micro) frame number in which it intends to transmit/receive isochronous data for this endpoint using the SetEvnFr and SetOddFr fields in this register.
 0: Even (micro)frame
 1: Odd (micro)frame
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field is reserved. The frame number in which to send data is provided in the transmit descriptor structure. The frame in which data is received is updated in receive descriptor structure.</description>
       <lsb>16</lsb>
       <msb>16</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl9_dpid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>naksts</name>
       <description>When either the application or the core sets this bit:
-The core stops receiving any data on an OUT endpoint, even if there is space in the RxFIFO to accommodate the incoming packet.
-for non-isochronous IN endpoints: The core stops transmitting any data on an IN endpoint, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
-for isochronous IN endpoints: The core sends out a zero-length data packet, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
       <lsb>17</lsb>
       <msb>17</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl9_naksts_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>nonnak</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>nak</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>eptype</name>
       <description>This is the transfer type supported by this logical endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>18</lsb>
       <msb>19</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl9_eptype_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>control</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>isochronous</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>bulk</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>interrup</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>snp</name>
       <description>Applies to OUT endpoints only. This bit configures the endpoint to Snoop mode. In Snoop mode, the core does not check the correctness of OUT packets before transferring them to application memory.</description>
       <lsb>20</lsb>
       <msb>20</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl9_snp_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disable</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enable</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>stall</name>
       <description>Applies to non-control, non-isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. The application sets this bit to stall all tokens from the USB host to this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Only the application can clear this bit, never the core. Applies to control endpoints only. The application can only set this bit, and the core clears it, when a SETUP token is received for this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
       <lsb>21</lsb>
       <msb>21</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl9_stall_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>cnak</name>
       <description>A write to this bit clears the NAK bit for the endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>26</lsb>
       <msb>26</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl9_cnak_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>snak</name>
       <description>A write to this bit sets the NAK bit for the endpoint. Using this bit, the application can control the transmission of NAK handshakes on an endpoint. The core can also Set this bit for an endpoint after a SETUP packet is received on that endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>27</lsb>
       <msb>27</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl9_snak_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setd0pid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA0. This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode. In non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode: Set Even (micro)frame (SetEvenFr) Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Even/Odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to even (micro) frame. When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field is reserved. The frame number in which to send data is in the transmit descriptor structure. The frame in which to receive data is updated in receive descriptor structure.</description>
       <lsb>28</lsb>
       <msb>28</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl9_setd0pid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setd1pid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA1. This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode.
Set Odd (micro)frame (SetOddFr) Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Even/Odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to odd (micro)frame.This field is not applicable for Scatter/Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>29</lsb>
       <msb>29</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl9_setd1pid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epdis</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. The application sets this bit to stop transmitting/receiving data on an endpoint, even before the transfer for that endpoint is complete. The application must wait for the Endpoint Disabled interrupt before treating the endpoint as disabled. The core clears this bit before setting the Endpoint Disabled interrupt. The application must set this bit only if Endpoint Enable is already set for this endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>30</lsb>
       <msb>30</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl9_epdis_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epena</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.
-When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled,
-for IN endpoints this bit indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer with data ready to transmit is setup.
-for OUT endpoint it indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer to receive data is setup.
-When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled such as for buffer-pointer based DMA mode:
- for IN endpoints, this bit indicates that data is ready to be transmitted on the endpoint.
- for OUT endpoints, this bit indicates that the application has allocated the memory to start receiving data from the USB.
- The core clears this bit before setting any of the following interrupts on this endpoint:
-SETUP Phase Done
-Endpoint Disabled
-Transfer Completed
for control endpoints in DMA mode, this bit must be set to be able to transfer SETUP data packets in memory.</description>
       <lsb>31</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl9_epena_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepint9</name>
     <description>This register indicates the status of an endpoint with respect to USB- and AHB-related events. The application must read this register when the OUT Endpoints Interrupt bit or IN Endpoints Interrupt bit of the Core Interrupt register (GINTSTS.OEPInt or GINTSTS.IEPInt, respectively) is set. Before the application can read this register, it must first read the Device All Endpoints Interrupt (DAINT) register to get the exact endpoint number for the Device Endpoint-n Interrupt register. The application must clear the appropriate bit in this register to clear the corresponding bits in the DAINT and GINTSTS registers.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc28</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>xfercompl</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled
This field indicates that the requested data from the internal FIFO is moved to external system memory. This interrupt is generated only when the corresponding endpoint descriptor is closed, and the IOC bit for the corresponding descriptor is Set.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is disabled, this field indicates that the programmed transfer is complete on the AHB as well as on the USB, for this endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint9_xfercompl_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epdisbld</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. This bit indicates that the endpoint is disabled per the application's request.</description>
       <lsb>1</lsb>
       <msb>1</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint9_epdisbld_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>ahberr</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.This is generated only in Internal DMA mode when there is an AHB error during an AHB read/write. The application can read the corresponding endpoint DMA address register to get the error address.</description>
       <lsb>2</lsb>
       <msb>2</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint9_ahberr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setup</name>
       <description>Applies to control OUT endpoints only. Indicates that the SETUP phase for the control endpoint is complete and no more back-to-back SETUP packets were received for the current control transfer. On this interrupt, the application can decode the received SETUP data packet.</description>
       <lsb>3</lsb>
       <msb>3</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint9_setup_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>outtknepdis</name>
       <description>Applies only to control OUT endpoints. Indicates that an OUT token was received when the endpoint was not yet enabled. This interrupt is asserted on the endpoint for which the OUT token was received.</description>
       <lsb>4</lsb>
       <msb>4</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint9_outtknepdis_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>stsphsercvd</name>
       <description>This interrupt is valid only for Control OUT endpoints and only in Scatter Gather DMA mode. This interrupt is generated only after the core has transferred all the data that the host has sent during the data phase of a control write transfer, to the system memory buffer. The interrupt indicates to the application that the host has switched from data phase to the status phase of a Control Write transfer. The application can use this interrupt to ACK or STALL the Status phase, after it has decoded the data phase. This is applicable only in Case of Scatter Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>5</lsb>
       <msb>5</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint9_stsphsercvd_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>back2backsetup</name>
       <description>Applies to Control OUT endpoints only. This bit indicates that the core has received more than three back-to-back SETUP packets for this particular endpoint. for information about handling this interrupt,</description>
       <lsb>6</lsb>
       <msb>6</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint9_back2backsetup_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>outpkterr</name>
       <description>Applies to OUT endpoints Only This interrupt is asserted when the core detects an overflow or a CRC error for non-Isochronous OUT packet.</description>
       <lsb>8</lsb>
       <msb>8</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint9_outpkterr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bnaintr</name>
       <description>This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled. The core generates this interrupt when the descriptor accessed is not ready for the Core to process, such as Host busy or DMA done</description>
       <lsb>9</lsb>
       <msb>9</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint9_bnaintr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>pktdrpsts</name>
       <description>This bit indicates to the application that an ISOC OUT packet has been dropped. This bit does not have an associated mask bit and does not generate an interrupt.</description>
       <lsb>11</lsb>
       <msb>11</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint9_pktdrpsts_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bbleerr</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when babble is received for the endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>12</lsb>
       <msb>12</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint9_bbleerr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>nakintrpt</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when a NAK is transmitted or received by the device. In case of isochronous IN endpoints the interrupt gets generated when a zero length packet is transmitted due to un-availability of data in the TXFifo.</description>
       <lsb>13</lsb>
       <msb>13</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint9_nakintrpt_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>nyetintrpt</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when a NYET response is transmitted for a non isochronous OUT endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>14</lsb>
       <msb>14</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint9_nyetintrpt_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doeptsiz9</name>
     <description>The application must modify this register before enabling the endpoint. Once the endpoint is enabled using Endpoint Enable bit of the Device Endpoint-n Control registers (DIEPCTLn.EPEna/DOEPCTLn.EPEna), the core modifies this register. The application can only read this register once the core has cleared the Endpoint Enable bit.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc30</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>xfersize</name>
       <description>Indicates the transfer size in bytes for endpoint 0. The core interrupts the application only after it has exhausted the transfer size amount of data. The transfer size can be Set to the maximum packet size of the endpoint, to be interrupted at the end of each packet. The core decrements this field every time a packet from the external memory is written to the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>18</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>pktcnt</name>
       <description>Indicates the total number of USB packets that constitute the Transfer Size amount of data for endpoint 0.This field is decremented every time a packet (maximum size or short packet) is read from the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>19</lsb>
       <msb>28</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>rxdpid</name>
       <description>Applies to isochronous OUT endpoints only.This is the data PID received in the last packet for this endpoint. Use datax.
Applies to control OUT Endpoints only. Use packetx. This field specifies the number of back-to-back SETUP data packets the endpoint can receive.</description>
       <lsb>29</lsb>
       <msb>30</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doeptsiz9_rxdpid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data0</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data2packet1</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data1packet2</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>mdatapacket3</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdma9</name>
     <description>DMA OUT Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc34</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdma9</name>
       <description>Holds the start address of the external memory for storing or fetching endpoint data. for control endpoints, this field stores control OUT data packets as well as SETUP transaction data packets. When more than three SETUP packets are received back-to-back, the SETUP data packet in the memory is overwritten. This register is incremented on every AHB transaction. The application can give only a DWORD-aligned address.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is not enabled, the application programs the start address value in this field.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field indicates the base pointer for the descriptor list.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdmab9</name>
     <description>DMA Buffer Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc3c</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdmab9</name>
       <description>Holds the current buffer address. This register is updated as and when the data transfer for the corresponding end point is in progress. This register is present only in Scatter/Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepctl10</name>
     <description>Out Endpoint 10.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc40</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>mps</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. The application must program this field with the maximum packet size for the current logical endpoint. This value is in bytes.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>10</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>usbactep</name>
       <description>Indicates whether this endpoint is active in the current configuration and interface. The core clears this bit for all endpoints (other than EP 0) after detecting a USB reset. After receiving the SetConfiguration and SetInterface commands, the application must program endpoint registers accordingly and set this bit.</description>
       <lsb>15</lsb>
       <msb>15</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl10_usbactep_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>dpid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Contains the PID of the packet to be received or transmitted on this endpoint. The application must program the PID of the first packet to be received or transmitted on this endpoint, after the endpoint is activated. The applications use the SetD1PID and SetD0PID fields of this register to program either DATA0 or DATA1 PID. 0: DATA0 1: DATA1This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode. Even/Odd (Micro)Frame (EO_FrNum) In non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode:
Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Indicates the (micro)frame number in which the core transmits/receives isochronous data for this endpoint. The application must program the even/odd (micro) frame number in which it intends to transmit/receive isochronous data for this endpoint using the SetEvnFr and SetOddFr fields in this register.
 0: Even (micro)frame
 1: Odd (micro)frame
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field is reserved. The frame number in which to send data is provided in the transmit descriptor structure. The frame in which data is received is updated in receive descriptor structure.</description>
       <lsb>16</lsb>
       <msb>16</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl10_dpid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>naksts</name>
       <description>When either the application or the core sets this bit:
-The core stops receiving any data on an OUT endpoint, even if there is space in the RxFIFO to accommodate the incoming packet.
-for non-isochronous IN endpoints: The core stops transmitting any data on an IN endpoint, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
-for isochronous IN endpoints: The core sends out a zero-length data packet, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
       <lsb>17</lsb>
       <msb>17</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl10_naksts_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>nonnak</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>nak</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>eptype</name>
       <description>This is the transfer type supported by this logical endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>18</lsb>
       <msb>19</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl10_eptype_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>control</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>isochronous</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>bulk</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>interrup</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>snp</name>
       <description>Applies to OUT endpoints only. This bit configures the endpoint to Snoop mode. In Snoop mode, the core does not check the correctness of OUT packets before transferring them to application memory.</description>
       <lsb>20</lsb>
       <msb>20</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl10_snp_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disable</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enable</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>stall</name>
       <description>Applies to non-control, non-isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. The application sets this bit to stall all tokens from the USB host to this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Only the application can clear this bit, never the core. Applies to control endpoints only. The application can only set this bit, and the core clears it, when a SETUP token is received for this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
       <lsb>21</lsb>
       <msb>21</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl10_stall_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>cnak</name>
       <description>A write to this bit clears the NAK bit for the endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>26</lsb>
       <msb>26</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl10_cnak_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>snak</name>
       <description>A write to this bit sets the NAK bit for the endpoint. Using this bit, the application can control the transmission of NAK handshakes on an endpoint. The core can also Set this bit for an endpoint after a SETUP packet is received on that endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>27</lsb>
       <msb>27</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl10_snak_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setd0pid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA0. This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode. In non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode: Set Even (micro)frame (SetEvenFr) Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Even/Odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to even (micro) frame. When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field is reserved. The frame number in which to send data is in the transmit descriptor structure. The frame in which to receive data is updated in receive descriptor structure.</description>
       <lsb>28</lsb>
       <msb>28</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl10_setd0pid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setd1pid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA1. This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode.
Set Odd (micro)frame (SetOddFr) Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Even/Odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to odd (micro)frame.This field is not applicable for Scatter/Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>29</lsb>
       <msb>29</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl10_setd1pid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epdis</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. The application sets this bit to stop transmitting/receiving data on an endpoint, even before the transfer for that endpoint is complete. The application must wait for the Endpoint Disabled interrupt before treating the endpoint as disabled. The core clears this bit before setting the Endpoint Disabled interrupt. The application must set this bit only if Endpoint Enable is already set for this endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>30</lsb>
       <msb>30</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl10_epdis_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epena</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.
-When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled,
-for IN endpoints this bit indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer with data ready to transmit is setup.
-for OUT endpoint it indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer to receive data is setup.
-When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled such as for buffer-pointer based DMA mode:
- for IN endpoints, this bit indicates that data is ready to be transmitted on the endpoint.
- for OUT endpoints, this bit indicates that the application has allocated the memory to start receiving data from the USB.
- The core clears this bit before setting any of the following interrupts on this endpoint:
-SETUP Phase Done
-Endpoint Disabled
-Transfer Completed
for control endpoints in DMA mode, this bit must be set to be able to transfer SETUP data packets in memory.</description>
       <lsb>31</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl10_epena_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepint10</name>
     <description>This register indicates the status of an endpoint with respect to USB- and AHB-related events. The application must read this register when the OUT Endpoints Interrupt bit or IN Endpoints Interrupt bit of the Core Interrupt register (GINTSTS.OEPInt or GINTSTS.IEPInt, respectively) is set. Before the application can read this register, it must first read the Device All Endpoints Interrupt (DAINT) register to get the exact endpoint number for the Device Endpoint-n Interrupt register. The application must clear the appropriate bit in this register to clear the corresponding bits in the DAINT and GINTSTS registers.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc48</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>xfercompl</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled
This field indicates that the requested data from the internal FIFO is moved to external system memory. This interrupt is generated only when the corresponding endpoint descriptor is closed, and the IOC bit for the corresponding descriptor is Set.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is disabled, this field indicates that the programmed transfer is complete on the AHB as well as on the USB, for this endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint10_xfercompl_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epdisbld</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. This bit indicates that the endpoint is disabled per the application's request.</description>
       <lsb>1</lsb>
       <msb>1</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint10_epdisbld_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>ahberr</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.This is generated only in Internal DMA mode when there is an AHB error during an AHB read/write. The application can read the corresponding endpoint DMA address register to get the error address.</description>
       <lsb>2</lsb>
       <msb>2</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint10_ahberr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setup</name>
       <description>Applies to control OUT endpoints only. Indicates that the SETUP phase for the control endpoint is complete and no more back-to-back SETUP packets were received for the current control transfer. On this interrupt, the application can decode the received SETUP data packet.</description>
       <lsb>3</lsb>
       <msb>3</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint10_setup_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>outtknepdis</name>
       <description>Applies only to control OUT endpoints. Indicates that an OUT token was received when the endpoint was not yet enabled. This interrupt is asserted on the endpoint for which the OUT token was received.</description>
       <lsb>4</lsb>
       <msb>4</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint10_outtknepdis_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>stsphsercvd</name>
       <description>This interrupt is valid only for Control OUT endpoints and only in Scatter Gather DMA mode. This interrupt is generated only after the core has transferred all the data that the host has sent during the data phase of a control write transfer, to the system memory buffer. The interrupt indicates to the application that the host has switched from data phase to the status phase of a Control Write transfer. The application can use this interrupt to ACK or STALL the Status phase, after it has decoded the data phase. This is applicable only in Case of Scatter Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>5</lsb>
       <msb>5</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint10_stsphsercvd_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>back2backsetup</name>
       <description>Applies to Control OUT endpoints only. This bit indicates that the core has received more than three back-to-back SETUP packets for this particular endpoint. for information about handling this interrupt,</description>
       <lsb>6</lsb>
       <msb>6</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint10_back2backsetup_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>outpkterr</name>
       <description>Applies to OUT endpoints Only This interrupt is asserted when the core detects an overflow or a CRC error for non-Isochronous OUT packet.</description>
       <lsb>8</lsb>
       <msb>8</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint10_outpkterr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bnaintr</name>
       <description>This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled. The core generates this interrupt when the descriptor accessed is not ready for the Core to process, such as Host busy or DMA done</description>
       <lsb>9</lsb>
       <msb>9</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint10_bnaintr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>pktdrpsts</name>
       <description>This bit indicates to the application that an ISOC OUT packet has been dropped. This bit does not have an associated mask bit and does not generate an interrupt.</description>
       <lsb>11</lsb>
       <msb>11</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint10_pktdrpsts_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bbleerr</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when babble is received for the endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>12</lsb>
       <msb>12</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint10_bbleerr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>nakintrpt</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when a NAK is transmitted or received by the device. In case of isochronous IN endpoints the interrupt gets generated when a zero length packet is transmitted due to un-availability of data in the TXFifo.</description>
       <lsb>13</lsb>
       <msb>13</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint10_nakintrpt_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>nyetintrpt</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when a NYET response is transmitted for a non isochronous OUT endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>14</lsb>
       <msb>14</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint10_nyetintrpt_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doeptsiz10</name>
     <description>The application must modify this register before enabling the endpoint. Once the endpoint is enabled using Endpoint Enable bit of the Device Endpoint-n Control registers (DIEPCTLn.EPEna/DOEPCTLn.EPEna), the core modifies this register. The application can only read this register once the core has cleared the Endpoint Enable bit.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc50</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>xfersize</name>
       <description>Indicates the transfer size in bytes for endpoint 0. The core interrupts the application only after it has exhausted the transfer size amount of data. The transfer size can be Set to the maximum packet size of the endpoint, to be interrupted at the end of each packet. The core decrements this field every time a packet from the external memory is written to the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>18</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>pktcnt</name>
       <description>Indicates the total number of USB packets that constitute the Transfer Size amount of data for endpoint 0.This field is decremented every time a packet (maximum size or short packet) is read from the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>19</lsb>
       <msb>28</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>rxdpid</name>
       <description>Applies to isochronous OUT endpoints only.This is the data PID received in the last packet for this endpoint. Use datax.
Applies to control OUT Endpoints only. Use packetx. This field specifies the number of back-to-back SETUP data packets the endpoint can receive.</description>
       <lsb>29</lsb>
       <msb>30</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doeptsiz10_rxdpid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data0</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data2packet1</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data1packet2</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>mdatapacket3</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdma10</name>
     <description>DMA OUT Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc54</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdma10</name>
       <description>Holds the start address of the external memory for storing or fetching endpoint data. for control endpoints, this field stores control OUT data packets as well as SETUP transaction data packets. When more than three SETUP packets are received back-to-back, the SETUP data packet in the memory is overwritten. This register is incremented on every AHB transaction. The application can give only a DWORD-aligned address.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is not enabled, the application programs the start address value in this field.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field indicates the base pointer for the descriptor list.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdmab10</name>
     <description>DMA Buffer Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc5c</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdmab10</name>
       <description>Holds the current buffer address. This register is updated as and when the data transfer for the corresponding end point is in progress. This register is present only in Scatter/Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepctl11</name>
     <description>Out Endpoint 11.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc60</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>mps</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. The application must program this field with the maximum packet size for the current logical endpoint. This value is in bytes.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>10</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>usbactep</name>
       <description>Indicates whether this endpoint is active in the current configuration and interface. The core clears this bit for all endpoints (other than EP 0) after detecting a USB reset. After receiving the SetConfiguration and SetInterface commands, the application must program endpoint registers accordingly and set this bit.</description>
       <lsb>15</lsb>
       <msb>15</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl11_usbactep_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>dpid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Contains the PID of the packet to be received or transmitted on this endpoint. The application must program the PID of the first packet to be received or transmitted on this endpoint, after the endpoint is activated. The applications use the SetD1PID and SetD0PID fields of this register to program either DATA0 or DATA1 PID.
0: DATA0
1: DATA1This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode. Even/Odd (Micro)Frame (EO_FrNum) In non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode:
Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Indicates the (micro)frame number in which the core transmits/receives isochronous data for this endpoint. The application must program the even/odd (micro) frame number in which it intends to transmit/receive isochronous data for this endpoint using the SetEvnFr and SetOddFr fields in this register.
0: Even (micro)frame
1: Odd (micro)frame
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field is reserved. The frame number in which to send data is provided in the transmit descriptor structure. The frame in which data is received is updated in receive descriptor structure.</description>
       <lsb>16</lsb>
       <msb>16</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl11_dpid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>naksts</name>
       <description>When either the application or the core sets this bit:
-The core stops receiving any data on an OUT endpoint, even if there is space in the RxFIFO to accommodate the incoming packet.
-for non-isochronous IN endpoints: The core stops transmitting any data on an IN endpoint, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
-for isochronous IN endpoints: The core sends out a zero-length data packet, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
       <lsb>17</lsb>
       <msb>17</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl11_naksts_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>nonnak</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>nak</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>eptype</name>
       <description>This is the transfer type supported by this logical endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>18</lsb>
       <msb>19</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl11_eptype_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>control</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>isochronous</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>bulk</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>interrup</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>snp</name>
       <description>Applies to OUT endpoints only. This bit configures the endpoint to Snoop mode. In Snoop mode, the core does not check the correctness of OUT packets before transferring them to application memory.</description>
       <lsb>20</lsb>
       <msb>20</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl11_snp_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disable</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enable</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>stall</name>
       <description>Applies to non-control, non-isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. The application sets this bit to stall all tokens from the USB host to this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Only the application can clear this bit, never the core. Applies to control endpoints only. The application can only set this bit, and the core clears it, when a SETUP token is received for this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
       <lsb>21</lsb>
       <msb>21</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl11_stall_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>cnak</name>
       <description>A write to this bit clears the NAK bit for the endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>26</lsb>
       <msb>26</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl11_cnak_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>snak</name>
       <description>A write to this bit sets the NAK bit for the endpoint. Using this bit, the application can control the transmission of NAK handshakes on an endpoint. The core can also Set this bit for an endpoint after a SETUP packet is received on that endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>27</lsb>
       <msb>27</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl11_snak_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setd0pid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA0. This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode. In non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode: Set Even (micro)frame (SetEvenFr) Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Even/Odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to even (micro) frame. When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field is reserved. The frame number in which to send data is in the transmit descriptor structure. The frame in which to receive data is updated in receive descriptor structure.</description>
       <lsb>28</lsb>
       <msb>28</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl11_setd0pid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setd1pid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA1. This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode.
Set Odd (micro)frame (SetOddFr) Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Even/Odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to odd (micro)frame.This field is not applicable for Scatter/Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>29</lsb>
       <msb>29</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl11_setd1pid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epdis</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. The application sets this bit to stop transmitting/receiving data on an endpoint, even before the transfer for that endpoint is complete. The application must wait for the Endpoint Disabled interrupt before treating the endpoint as disabled. The core clears this bit before setting the Endpoint Disabled interrupt. The application must set this bit only if Endpoint Enable is already set for this endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>30</lsb>
       <msb>30</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl11_epdis_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epena</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.
-When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled,
-for IN endpoints this bit indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer with data ready to transmit is setup.
-for OUT endpoint it indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer to receive data is setup.
-When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled such as for buffer-pointer based DMA mode:
- for IN endpoints, this bit indicates that data is ready to be transmitted on the endpoint.
- for OUT endpoints, this bit indicates that the application has allocated the memory to start receiving data from the USB.
- The core clears this bit before setting any of the following interrupts on this endpoint:
-SETUP Phase Done
-Endpoint Disabled
-Transfer Completed
for control endpoints in DMA mode, this bit must be set to be able to transfer SETUP data packets in memory.</description>
       <lsb>31</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl11_epena_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepint11</name>
     <description>This register indicates the status of an endpoint with respect to USB- and AHB-related events. The application must read this register when the OUT Endpoints Interrupt bit or IN Endpoints Interrupt bit of the Core Interrupt register (GINTSTS.OEPInt or GINTSTS.IEPInt, respectively) is set. Before the application can read this register, it must first read the Device All Endpoints Interrupt (DAINT) register to get the exact endpoint number for the Device Endpoint-n Interrupt register. The application must clear the appropriate bit in this register to clear the corresponding bits in the DAINT and GINTSTS registers.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc68</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>xfercompl</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled
This field indicates that the requested data from the internal FIFO is moved to external system memory. This interrupt is generated only when the corresponding endpoint descriptor is closed, and the IOC bit for the corresponding descriptor is Set.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is disabled, this field indicates that the programmed transfer is complete on the AHB as well as on the USB, for this endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint11_xfercompl_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epdisbld</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. This bit indicates that the endpoint is disabled per the application's request.</description>
       <lsb>1</lsb>
       <msb>1</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint11_epdisbld_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>ahberr</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.This is generated only in Internal DMA mode when there is an AHB error during an AHB read/write. The application can read the corresponding endpoint DMA address register to get the error address.</description>
       <lsb>2</lsb>
       <msb>2</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint11_ahberr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setup</name>
       <description>Applies to control OUT endpoints only. Indicates that the SETUP phase for the control endpoint is complete and no more back-to-back SETUP packets were received for the current control transfer. On this interrupt, the application can decode the received SETUP data packet.</description>
       <lsb>3</lsb>
       <msb>3</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint11_setup_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>outtknepdis</name>
       <description>Applies only to control OUT endpoints. Indicates that an OUT token was received when the endpoint was not yet enabled. This interrupt is asserted on the endpoint for which the OUT token was received.</description>
       <lsb>4</lsb>
       <msb>4</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint11_outtknepdis_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>stsphsercvd</name>
       <description>This interrupt is valid only for Control OUT endpoints and only in Scatter Gather DMA mode. This interrupt is generated only after the core has transferred all the data that the host has sent during the data phase of a control write transfer, to the system memory buffer. The interrupt indicates to the application that the host has switched from data phase to the status phase of a Control Write transfer. The application can use this interrupt to ACK or STALL the Status phase, after it has decoded the data phase. This is applicable only in Case of Scatter Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>5</lsb>
       <msb>5</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint11_stsphsercvd_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>back2backsetup</name>
       <description>Applies to Control OUT endpoints only. This bit indicates that the core has received more than three back-to-back SETUP packets for this particular endpoint. for information about handling this interrupt,</description>
       <lsb>6</lsb>
       <msb>6</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint11_back2backsetup_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>outpkterr</name>
       <description>Applies to OUT endpoints Only This interrupt is asserted when the core detects an overflow or a CRC error for non-Isochronous OUT packet.</description>
       <lsb>8</lsb>
       <msb>8</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint11_outpkterr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bnaintr</name>
       <description>This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled. The core generates this interrupt when the descriptor accessed is not ready for the Core to process, such as Host busy or DMA done</description>
       <lsb>9</lsb>
       <msb>9</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint11_bnaintr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>pktdrpsts</name>
       <description>This bit indicates to the application that an ISOC OUT packet has been dropped. This bit does not have an associated mask bit and does not generate an interrupt.</description>
       <lsb>11</lsb>
       <msb>11</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint11_pktdrpsts_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bbleerr</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when babble is received for the endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>12</lsb>
       <msb>12</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint11_bbleerr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>nakintrpt</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when a NAK is transmitted or received by the device. In case of isochronous IN endpoints the interrupt gets generated when a zero length packet is transmitted due to un-availability of data in the TXFifo.</description>
       <lsb>13</lsb>
       <msb>13</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint11_nakintrpt_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>nyetintrpt</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when a NYET response is transmitted for a non isochronous OUT endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>14</lsb>
       <msb>14</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint11_nyetintrpt_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doeptsiz11</name>
     <description>The application must modify this register before enabling the endpoint. Once the endpoint is enabled using Endpoint Enable bit of the Device Endpoint-n Control registers (DIEPCTLn.EPEna/DOEPCTLn.EPEna), the core modifies this register. The application can only read this register once the core has cleared the Endpoint Enable bit.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc70</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>xfersize</name>
       <description>Indicates the transfer size in bytes for endpoint 0. The core interrupts the application only after it has exhausted the transfer size amount of data. The transfer size can be Set to the maximum packet size of the endpoint, to be interrupted at the end of each packet. The core decrements this field every time a packet from the external memory is written to the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>18</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>pktcnt</name>
       <description>Indicates the total number of USB packets that constitute the Transfer Size amount of data for endpoint 0.This field is decremented every time a packet (maximum size or short packet) is read from the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>19</lsb>
       <msb>28</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>rxdpid</name>
       <description>Applies to isochronous OUT endpoints only.This is the data PID received in the last packet for this endpoint. Use datax.
Applies to control OUT Endpoints only. Use packetx. This field specifies the number of back-to-back SETUP data packets the endpoint can receive.</description>
       <lsb>29</lsb>
       <msb>30</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doeptsiz11_rxdpid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data0</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data2packet1</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data1packet2</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>mdatapacket3</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdma11</name>
     <description>DMA OUT Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc74</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdma11</name>
       <description>Holds the start address of the external memory for storing or fetching endpoint data. for control endpoints, this field stores control OUT data packets as well as SETUP transaction data packets. When more than three SETUP packets are received back-to-back, the SETUP data packet in the memory is overwritten. This register is incremented on every AHB transaction. The application can give only a DWORD-aligned address.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is not enabled, the application programs the start address value in this field.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field indicates the base pointer for the descriptor list.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdmab11</name>
     <description>DMA Buffer Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc7c</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdmab11</name>
       <description>Holds the current buffer address. This register is updated as and when the data transfer for the corresponding end point is in progress. This register is present only in Scatter/Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepctl12</name>
     <description>Out Endpoint 12.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc80</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>mps</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. The application must program this field with the maximum packet size for the current logical endpoint. This value is in bytes.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>10</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>usbactep</name>
       <description>Indicates whether this endpoint is active in the current configuration and interface. The core clears this bit for all endpoints (other than EP 0) after detecting a USB reset. After receiving the SetConfiguration and SetInterface commands, the application must program endpoint registers accordingly and set this bit.</description>
       <lsb>15</lsb>
       <msb>15</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl12_usbactep_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>dpid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Contains the PID of the packet to be received or transmitted on this endpoint. The application must program the PID of the first packet to be received or transmitted on this endpoint, after the endpoint is activated. The applications use the SetD1PID and SetD0PID fields of this register to program either DATA0 or DATA1 PID.
0: DATA0
1: DATA1This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode. Even/Odd (Micro)Frame (EO_FrNum) In non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode:
Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Indicates the (micro)frame number in which the core transmits/receives isochronous data for this endpoint. The application must program the even/odd (micro) frame number in which it intends to transmit/receive isochronous data for this endpoint using the SetEvnFr and SetOddFr fields in this register.
0: Even (micro)frame
1: Odd (micro)frame
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field is reserved. The frame number in which to send data is provided in the transmit descriptor structure. The frame in which data is received is updated in receive descriptor structure.</description>
       <lsb>16</lsb>
       <msb>16</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl12_dpid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>naksts</name>
       <description>When either the application or the core sets this bit:
-The core stops receiving any data on an OUT endpoint, even if there is space in the RxFIFO to accommodate the incoming packet.
-for non-isochronous IN endpoints: The core stops transmitting any data on an IN endpoint, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
-for isochronous IN endpoints: The core sends out a zero-length data packet, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
       <lsb>17</lsb>
       <msb>17</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl12_naksts_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>nonnak</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>nak</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>eptype</name>
       <description>This is the transfer type supported by this logical endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>18</lsb>
       <msb>19</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl12_eptype_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>control</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>isochronous</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>bulk</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>interrup</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>snp</name>
       <description>Applies to OUT endpoints only. This bit configures the endpoint to Snoop mode. In Snoop mode, the core does not check the correctness of OUT packets before transferring them to application memory.</description>
       <lsb>20</lsb>
       <msb>20</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl12_snp_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disable</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enable</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>stall</name>
       <description>Applies to non-control, non-isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. The application sets this bit to stall all tokens from the USB host to this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Only the application can clear this bit, never the core. Applies to control endpoints only. The application can only set this bit, and the core clears it, when a SETUP token is received for this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
       <lsb>21</lsb>
       <msb>21</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl12_stall_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>cnak</name>
       <description>A write to this bit clears the NAK bit for the endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>26</lsb>
       <msb>26</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl12_cnak_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>snak</name>
       <description>A write to this bit sets the NAK bit for the endpoint. Using this bit, the application can control the transmission of NAK handshakes on an endpoint. The core can also Set this bit for an endpoint after a SETUP packet is received on that endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>27</lsb>
       <msb>27</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl12_snak_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setd0pid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA0. This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode. In non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode: Set Even (micro)frame (SetEvenFr) Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Even/Odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to even (micro) frame. When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field is reserved. The frame number in which to send data is in the transmit descriptor structure. The frame in which to receive data is updated in receive descriptor structure.</description>
       <lsb>28</lsb>
       <msb>28</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl12_setd0pid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setd1pid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA1. This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode.
Set Odd (micro)frame (SetOddFr) Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Even/Odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to odd (micro)frame.This field is not applicable for Scatter/Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>29</lsb>
       <msb>29</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl12_setd1pid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epdis</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. The application sets this bit to stop transmitting/receiving data on an endpoint, even before the transfer for that endpoint is complete. The application must wait for the Endpoint Disabled interrupt before treating the endpoint as disabled. The core clears this bit before setting the Endpoint Disabled interrupt. The application must set this bit only if Endpoint Enable is already set for this endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>30</lsb>
       <msb>30</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl12_epdis_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epena</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.
-When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled,
-for IN endpoints this bit indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer with data ready to transmit is setup.
-for OUT endpoint it indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer to receive data is setup.
-When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled such as for buffer-pointer based DMA mode:
- for IN endpoints, this bit indicates that data is ready to be transmitted on the endpoint.
- for OUT endpoints, this bit indicates that the application has allocated the memory to start receiving data from the USB.
- The core clears this bit before setting any of the following interrupts on this endpoint:
-SETUP Phase Done
-Endpoint Disabled
-Transfer Completed
for control endpoints in DMA mode, this bit must be set to be able to transfer SETUP data packets in memory.</description>
       <lsb>31</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl12_epena_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepint12</name>
     <description>This register indicates the status of an endpoint with respect to USB- and AHB-related events. The application must read this register when the OUT Endpoints Interrupt bit or IN Endpoints Interrupt bit of the Core Interrupt register (GINTSTS.OEPInt or GINTSTS.IEPInt, respectively) is set. Before the application can read this register, it must first read the Device All Endpoints Interrupt (DAINT) register to get the exact endpoint number for the Device Endpoint-n Interrupt register. The application must clear the appropriate bit in this register to clear the corresponding bits in the DAINT and GINTSTS registers.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc88</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>xfercompl</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled
This field indicates that the requested data from the internal FIFO is moved to external system memory. This interrupt is generated only when the corresponding endpoint descriptor is closed, and the IOC bit for the corresponding descriptor is Set.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is disabled, this field indicates that the programmed transfer is complete on the AHB as well as on the USB, for this endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint12_xfercompl_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epdisbld</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. This bit indicates that the endpoint is disabled per the application's request.</description>
       <lsb>1</lsb>
       <msb>1</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint12_epdisbld_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>ahberr</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.This is generated only in Internal DMA mode when there is an AHB error during an AHB read/write. The application can read the corresponding endpoint DMA address register to get the error address.</description>
       <lsb>2</lsb>
       <msb>2</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint12_ahberr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setup</name>
       <description>Applies to control OUT endpoints only. Indicates that the SETUP phase for the control endpoint is complete and no more back-to-back SETUP packets were received for the current control transfer. On this interrupt, the application can decode the received SETUP data packet.</description>
       <lsb>3</lsb>
       <msb>3</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint12_setup_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>outtknepdis</name>
       <description>Applies only to control OUT endpoints. Indicates that an OUT token was received when the endpoint was not yet enabled. This interrupt is asserted on the endpoint for which the OUT token was received.</description>
       <lsb>4</lsb>
       <msb>4</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint12_outtknepdis_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>stsphsercvd</name>
       <description>This interrupt is valid only for Control OUT endpoints and only in Scatter Gather DMA mode. This interrupt is generated only after the core has transferred all the data that the host has sent during the data phase of a control write transfer, to the system memory buffer. The interrupt indicates to the application that the host has switched from data phase to the status phase of a Control Write transfer. The application can use this interrupt to ACK or STALL the Status phase, after it has decoded the data phase. This is applicable only in Case of Scatter Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>5</lsb>
       <msb>5</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint12_stsphsercvd_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>back2backsetup</name>
       <description>Applies to Control OUT endpoints only. This bit indicates that the core has received more than three back-to-back SETUP packets for this particular endpoint. for information about handling this interrupt,</description>
       <lsb>6</lsb>
       <msb>6</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint12_back2backsetup_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>outpkterr</name>
       <description>Applies to OUT endpoints Only This interrupt is asserted when the core detects an overflow or a CRC error for non-Isochronous OUT packet.</description>
       <lsb>8</lsb>
       <msb>8</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint12_outpkterr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bnaintr</name>
       <description>This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled. The core generates this interrupt when the descriptor accessed is not ready for the Core to process, such as Host busy or DMA done</description>
       <lsb>9</lsb>
       <msb>9</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint12_bnaintr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>pktdrpsts</name>
       <description>This bit indicates to the application that an ISOC OUT packet has been dropped. This bit does not have an associated mask bit and does not generate an interrupt.</description>
       <lsb>11</lsb>
       <msb>11</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint12_pktdrpsts_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bbleerr</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when babble is received for the endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>12</lsb>
       <msb>12</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint12_bbleerr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>nakintrpt</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when a NAK is transmitted or received by the device. In case of isochronous IN endpoints the interrupt gets generated when a zero length packet is transmitted due to un-availability of data in the TXFifo.</description>
       <lsb>13</lsb>
       <msb>13</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint12_nakintrpt_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>nyetintrpt</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when a NYET response is transmitted for a non isochronous OUT endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>14</lsb>
       <msb>14</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint12_nyetintrpt_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doeptsiz12</name>
     <description>The application must modify this register before enabling the endpoint. Once the endpoint is enabled using Endpoint Enable bit of the Device Endpoint-n Control registers (DIEPCTLn.EPEna/DOEPCTLn.EPEna), the core modifies this register. The application can only read this register once the core has cleared the Endpoint Enable bit.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc90</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>xfersize</name>
       <description>Indicates the transfer size in bytes for endpoint 0. The core interrupts the application only after it has exhausted the transfer size amount of data. The transfer size can be Set to the maximum packet size of the endpoint, to be interrupted at the end of each packet. The core decrements this field every time a packet from the external memory is written to the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>18</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>pktcnt</name>
       <description>Indicates the total number of USB packets that constitute the Transfer Size amount of data for endpoint 0.This field is decremented every time a packet (maximum size or short packet) is read from the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>19</lsb>
       <msb>28</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>rxdpid</name>
       <description>Applies to isochronous OUT endpoints only.This is the data PID received in the last packet for this endpoint. Use datax.
Applies to control OUT Endpoints only. Use packetx. This field specifies the number of back-to-back SETUP data packets the endpoint can receive.</description>
       <lsb>29</lsb>
       <msb>30</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doeptsiz12_rxdpid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data0</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data2packet1</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data1packet2</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>mdatapacket3</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdma12</name>
     <description>DMA OUT Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc94</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdma12</name>
       <description>Holds the start address of the external memory for storing or fetching endpoint data. for control endpoints, this field stores control OUT data packets as well as SETUP transaction data packets. When more than three SETUP packets are received back-to-back, the SETUP data packet in the memory is overwritten. This register is incremented on every AHB transaction. The application can give only a DWORD-aligned address.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is not enabled, the application programs the start address value in this field.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field indicates the base pointer for the descriptor list.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdmab12</name>
     <description>DMA Buffer Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xc9c</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdmab12</name>
       <description>Holds the current buffer address. This register is updated as and when the data transfer for the corresponding end point is in progress. This register is present only in Scatter/Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepctl13</name>
     <description>Out Endpoint 13.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xca0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>mps</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. The application must program this field with the maximum packet size for the current logical endpoint. This value is in bytes.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>10</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>usbactep</name>
       <description>Indicates whether this endpoint is active in the current configuration and interface. The core clears this bit for all endpoints (other than EP 0) after detecting a USB reset. After receiving the SetConfiguration and SetInterface commands, the application must program endpoint registers accordingly and set this bit.</description>
       <lsb>15</lsb>
       <msb>15</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl13_usbactep_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>dpid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Contains the PID of the packet to be received or transmitted on this endpoint. The application must program the PID of the first packet to be received or transmitted on this endpoint, after the endpoint is activated. The applications use the SetD1PID and SetD0PID fields of this register to program either DATA0 or DATA1 PID.
0: DATA0
1: DATA1This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode. Even/Odd (Micro)Frame (EO_FrNum) In non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode:
Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Indicates the (micro)frame number in which the core transmits/receives isochronous data for this endpoint. The application must program the even/odd (micro) frame number in which it intends to transmit/receive isochronous data for this endpoint using the SetEvnFr and SetOddFr fields in this register.
0: Even (micro)frame
1: Odd (micro)frame
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field is reserved. The frame number in which to send data is provided in the transmit descriptor structure. The frame in which data is received is updated in receive descriptor structure.</description>
       <lsb>16</lsb>
       <msb>16</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl13_dpid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>naksts</name>
       <description>When either the application or the core sets this bit:
-The core stops receiving any data on an OUT endpoint, even if there is space in the RxFIFO to accommodate the incoming packet.
-for non-isochronous IN endpoints: The core stops transmitting any data on an IN endpoint, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
-for isochronous IN endpoints: The core sends out a zero-length data packet, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
       <lsb>17</lsb>
       <msb>17</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl13_naksts_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>nonnak</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>nak</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>eptype</name>
       <description>This is the transfer type supported by this logical endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>18</lsb>
       <msb>19</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl13_eptype_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>control</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>isochronous</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>bulk</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>interrup</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>snp</name>
       <description>Applies to OUT endpoints only. This bit configures the endpoint to Snoop mode. In Snoop mode, the core does not check the correctness of OUT packets before transferring them to application memory.</description>
       <lsb>20</lsb>
       <msb>20</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl13_snp_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disable</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enable</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>stall</name>
       <description>Applies to non-control, non-isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. The application sets this bit to stall all tokens from the USB host to this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Only the application can clear this bit, never the core. Applies to control endpoints only. The application can only set this bit, and the core clears it, when a SETUP token is received for this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
       <lsb>21</lsb>
       <msb>21</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl13_stall_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>cnak</name>
       <description>A write to this bit clears the NAK bit for the endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>26</lsb>
       <msb>26</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl13_cnak_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>snak</name>
       <description>A write to this bit sets the NAK bit for the endpoint. Using this bit, the application can control the transmission of NAK handshakes on an endpoint. The core can also Set this bit for an endpoint after a SETUP packet is received on that endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>27</lsb>
       <msb>27</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl13_snak_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setd0pid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA0. This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode. In non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode: Set Even (micro)frame (SetEvenFr) Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Even/Odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to even (micro) frame. When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field is reserved. The frame number in which to send data is in the transmit descriptor structure. The frame in which to receive data is updated in receive descriptor structure.</description>
       <lsb>28</lsb>
       <msb>28</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl13_setd0pid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setd1pid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA1. This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode.
Set Odd (micro)frame (SetOddFr) Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Even/Odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to odd (micro)frame.This field is not applicable for Scatter/Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>29</lsb>
       <msb>29</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl13_setd1pid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epdis</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. The application sets this bit to stop transmitting/receiving data on an endpoint, even before the transfer for that endpoint is complete. The application must wait for the Endpoint Disabled interrupt before treating the endpoint as disabled. The core clears this bit before setting the Endpoint Disabled interrupt. The application must set this bit only if Endpoint Enable is already set for this endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>30</lsb>
       <msb>30</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl13_epdis_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epena</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.
-When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled,
-for IN endpoints this bit indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer with data ready to transmit is setup.
-for OUT endpoint it indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer to receive data is setup.
-When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled such as for buffer-pointer based DMA mode:
- for IN endpoints, this bit indicates that data is ready to be transmitted on the endpoint.
- for OUT endpoints, this bit indicates that the application has allocated the memory to start receiving data from the USB.
- The core clears this bit before setting any of the following interrupts on this endpoint:
-SETUP Phase Done
-Endpoint Disabled
-Transfer Completed
for control endpoints in DMA mode, this bit must be set to be able to transfer SETUP data packets in memory.</description>
       <lsb>31</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl13_epena_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepint13</name>
     <description>This register indicates the status of an endpoint with respect to USB- and AHB-related events. The application must read this register when the OUT Endpoints Interrupt bit or IN Endpoints Interrupt bit of the Core Interrupt register (GINTSTS.OEPInt or GINTSTS.IEPInt, respectively) is set. Before the application can read this register, it must first read the Device All Endpoints Interrupt (DAINT) register to get the exact endpoint number for the Device Endpoint-n Interrupt register. The application must clear the appropriate bit in this register to clear the corresponding bits in the DAINT and GINTSTS registers.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xca8</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>xfercompl</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled
This field indicates that the requested data from the internal FIFO is moved to external system memory. This interrupt is generated only when the corresponding endpoint descriptor is closed, and the IOC bit for the corresponding descriptor is Set.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is disabled, this field indicates that the programmed transfer is complete on the AHB as well as on the USB, for this endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint13_xfercompl_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epdisbld</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. This bit indicates that the endpoint is disabled per the application's request.</description>
       <lsb>1</lsb>
       <msb>1</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint13_epdisbld_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>ahberr</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.This is generated only in Internal DMA mode when there is an AHB error during an AHB read/write. The application can read the corresponding endpoint DMA address register to get the error address.</description>
       <lsb>2</lsb>
       <msb>2</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint13_ahberr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setup</name>
       <description>Applies to control OUT endpoints only. Indicates that the SETUP phase for the control endpoint is complete and no more back-to-back SETUP packets were received for the current control transfer. On this interrupt, the application can decode the received SETUP data packet.</description>
       <lsb>3</lsb>
       <msb>3</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint13_setup_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>outtknepdis</name>
       <description>Applies only to control OUT endpoints. Indicates that an OUT token was received when the endpoint was not yet enabled. This interrupt is asserted on the endpoint for which the OUT token was received.</description>
       <lsb>4</lsb>
       <msb>4</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint13_outtknepdis_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>stsphsercvd</name>
       <description>This interrupt is valid only for Control OUT endpoints and only in Scatter Gather DMA mode. This interrupt is generated only after the core has transferred all the data that the host has sent during the data phase of a control write transfer, to the system memory buffer. The interrupt indicates to the application that the host has switched from data phase to the status phase of a Control Write transfer. The application can use this interrupt to ACK or STALL the Status phase, after it has decoded the data phase. This is applicable only in Case of Scatter Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>5</lsb>
       <msb>5</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint13_stsphsercvd_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>back2backsetup</name>
       <description>Applies to Control OUT endpoints only. This bit indicates that the core has received more than three back-to-back SETUP packets for this particular endpoint. for information about handling this interrupt,</description>
       <lsb>6</lsb>
       <msb>6</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint13_back2backsetup_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>outpkterr</name>
       <description>Applies to OUT endpoints Only This interrupt is asserted when the core detects an overflow or a CRC error for non-Isochronous OUT packet.</description>
       <lsb>8</lsb>
       <msb>8</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint13_outpkterr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bnaintr</name>
       <description>This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled. The core generates this interrupt when the descriptor accessed is not ready for the Core to process, such as Host busy or DMA done</description>
       <lsb>9</lsb>
       <msb>9</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint13_bnaintr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>pktdrpsts</name>
       <description>This bit indicates to the application that an ISOC OUT packet has been dropped. This bit does not have an associated mask bit and does not generate an interrupt.</description>
       <lsb>11</lsb>
       <msb>11</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint13_pktdrpsts_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bbleerr</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when babble is received for the endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>12</lsb>
       <msb>12</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint13_bbleerr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>nakintrpt</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when a NAK is transmitted or received by the device. In case of isochronous IN endpoints the interrupt gets generated when a zero length packet is transmitted due to un-availability of data in the TXFifo.</description>
       <lsb>13</lsb>
       <msb>13</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint13_nakintrpt_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>nyetintrpt</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when a NYET response is transmitted for a non isochronous OUT endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>14</lsb>
       <msb>14</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint13_nyetintrpt_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doeptsiz13</name>
     <description>The application must modify this register before enabling the endpoint. Once the endpoint is enabled using Endpoint Enable bit of the Device Endpoint-n Control registers (DIEPCTLn.EPEna/DOEPCTLn.EPEna), the core modifies this register. The application can only read this register once the core has cleared the Endpoint Enable bit.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xcb0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>xfersize</name>
       <description>Indicates the transfer size in bytes for endpoint 0. The core interrupts the application only after it has exhausted the transfer size amount of data. The transfer size can be Set to the maximum packet size of the endpoint, to be interrupted at the end of each packet. The core decrements this field every time a packet from the external memory is written to the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>18</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>pktcnt</name>
       <description>Indicates the total number of USB packets that constitute the Transfer Size amount of data for endpoint 0.This field is decremented every time a packet (maximum size or short packet) is read from the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>19</lsb>
       <msb>28</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>rxdpid</name>
       <description>Applies to isochronous OUT endpoints only.This is the data PID received in the last packet for this endpoint. Use datax.
Applies to control OUT Endpoints only. Use packetx. This field specifies the number of back-to-back SETUP data packets the endpoint can receive.</description>
       <lsb>29</lsb>
       <msb>30</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doeptsiz13_rxdpid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data0</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data2packet1</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data1packet2</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>mdatapacket3</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdma13</name>
     <description>DMA OUT Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xcb4</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdma13</name>
       <description>Holds the start address of the external memory for storing or fetching endpoint data. for control endpoints, this field stores control OUT data packets as well as SETUP transaction data packets. When more than three SETUP packets are received back-to-back, the SETUP data packet in the memory is overwritten. This register is incremented on every AHB transaction. The application can give only a DWORD-aligned address.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is not enabled, the application programs the start address value in this field.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field indicates the base pointer for the descriptor list.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdmab13</name>
     <description>DMA Buffer Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xcbc</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdmab13</name>
       <description>Holds the current buffer address. This register is updated as and when the data transfer for the corresponding end point is in progress. This register is present only in Scatter/Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepctl14</name>
     <description>Out Endpoint 14.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xcc0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>mps</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. The application must program this field with the maximum packet size for the current logical endpoint. This value is in bytes.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>10</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>usbactep</name>
       <description>Indicates whether this endpoint is active in the current configuration and interface. The core clears this bit for all endpoints (other than EP 0) after detecting a USB reset. After receiving the SetConfiguration and SetInterface commands, the application must program endpoint registers accordingly and set this bit.</description>
       <lsb>15</lsb>
       <msb>15</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl14_usbactep_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>dpid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Contains the PID of the packet to be received or transmitted on this endpoint. The application must program the PID of the first packet to be received or transmitted on this endpoint, after the endpoint is activated. The applications use the SetD1PID and SetD0PID fields of this register to program either DATA0 or DATA1 PID.
0: DATA0
1: DATA1This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode. Even/Odd (Micro)Frame (EO_FrNum) In non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode:
Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Indicates the (micro)frame number in which the core transmits/receives isochronous data for this endpoint. The application must program the even/odd (micro) frame number in which it intends to transmit/receive isochronous data for this endpoint using the SetEvnFr and SetOddFr fields in this register.
0: Even (micro)frame
1: Odd (micro)frame
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field is reserved. The frame number in which to send data is provided in the transmit descriptor structure. The frame in which data is received is updated in receive descriptor structure.</description>
       <lsb>16</lsb>
       <msb>16</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl14_dpid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>naksts</name>
       <description>When either the application or the core sets this bit:
-The core stops receiving any data on an OUT endpoint, even if there is space in the RxFIFO to accommodate the incoming packet.
-for non-isochronous IN endpoints: The core stops transmitting any data on an IN endpoint, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
-for isochronous IN endpoints: The core sends out a zero-length data packet, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
       <lsb>17</lsb>
       <msb>17</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl14_naksts_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>nonnak</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>nak</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>eptype</name>
       <description>This is the transfer type supported by this logical endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>18</lsb>
       <msb>19</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl14_eptype_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>control</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>isochronous</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>bulk</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>interrup</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>snp</name>
       <description>Applies to OUT endpoints only. This bit configures the endpoint to Snoop mode. In Snoop mode, the core does not check the correctness of OUT packets before transferring them to application memory.</description>
       <lsb>20</lsb>
       <msb>20</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl14_snp_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disable</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enable</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>stall</name>
       <description>Applies to non-control, non-isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. The application sets this bit to stall all tokens from the USB host to this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Only the application can clear this bit, never the core. Applies to control endpoints only. The application can only set this bit, and the core clears it, when a SETUP token is received for this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
       <lsb>21</lsb>
       <msb>21</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl14_stall_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>cnak</name>
       <description>A write to this bit clears the NAK bit for the endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>26</lsb>
       <msb>26</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl14_cnak_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>snak</name>
       <description>A write to this bit sets the NAK bit for the endpoint. Using this bit, the application can control the transmission of NAK handshakes on an endpoint. The core can also Set this bit for an endpoint after a SETUP packet is received on that endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>27</lsb>
       <msb>27</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl14_snak_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setd0pid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA0. This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode. In non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode: Set Even (micro)frame (SetEvenFr) Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Even/Odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to even (micro) frame. When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field is reserved. The frame number in which to send data is in the transmit descriptor structure. The frame in which to receive data is updated in receive descriptor structure.</description>
       <lsb>28</lsb>
       <msb>28</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl14_setd0pid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setd1pid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA1. This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode.
Set Odd (micro)frame (SetOddFr) Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Even/Odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to odd (micro)frame.This field is not applicable for Scatter/Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>29</lsb>
       <msb>29</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl14_setd1pid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epdis</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. The application sets this bit to stop transmitting/receiving data on an endpoint, even before the transfer for that endpoint is complete. The application must wait for the Endpoint Disabled interrupt before treating the endpoint as disabled. The core clears this bit before setting the Endpoint Disabled interrupt. The application must set this bit only if Endpoint Enable is already set for this endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>30</lsb>
       <msb>30</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl14_epdis_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epena</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.
-When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled,
-for IN endpoints this bit indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer with data ready to transmit is setup.
-for OUT endpoint it indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer to receive data is setup.
-When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled such as for buffer-pointer based DMA mode:
- for IN endpoints, this bit indicates that data is ready to be transmitted on the endpoint.
- for OUT endpoints, this bit indicates that the application has allocated the memory to start receiving data from the USB.
- The core clears this bit before setting any of the following interrupts on this endpoint:
-SETUP Phase Done
-Endpoint Disabled
-Transfer Completed
for control endpoints in DMA mode, this bit must be set to be able to transfer SETUP data packets in memory.</description>
       <lsb>31</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl14_epena_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepint14</name>
     <description>This register indicates the status of an endpoint with respect to USB- and AHB-related events. The application must read this register when the OUT Endpoints Interrupt bit or IN Endpoints Interrupt bit of the Core Interrupt register (GINTSTS.OEPInt or GINTSTS.IEPInt, respectively) is set. Before the application can read this register, it must first read the Device All Endpoints Interrupt (DAINT) register to get the exact endpoint number for the Device Endpoint-n Interrupt register. The application must clear the appropriate bit in this register to clear the corresponding bits in the DAINT and GINTSTS registers.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xcc8</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>xfercompl</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled
This field indicates that the requested data from the internal FIFO is moved to external system memory. This interrupt is generated only when the corresponding endpoint descriptor is closed, and the IOC bit for the corresponding descriptor is Set.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is disabled, this field indicates that the programmed transfer is complete on the AHB as well as on the USB, for this endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint14_xfercompl_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epdisbld</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. This bit indicates that the endpoint is disabled per the application's request.</description>
       <lsb>1</lsb>
       <msb>1</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint14_epdisbld_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>ahberr</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.This is generated only in Internal DMA mode when there is an AHB error during an AHB read/write. The application can read the corresponding endpoint DMA address register to get the error address.</description>
       <lsb>2</lsb>
       <msb>2</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint14_ahberr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setup</name>
       <description>Applies to control OUT endpoints only. Indicates that the SETUP phase for the control endpoint is complete and no more back-to-back SETUP packets were received for the current control transfer. On this interrupt, the application can decode the received SETUP data packet.</description>
       <lsb>3</lsb>
       <msb>3</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint14_setup_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>outtknepdis</name>
       <description>Applies only to control OUT endpoints. Indicates that an OUT token was received when the endpoint was not yet enabled. This interrupt is asserted on the endpoint for which the OUT token was received.</description>
       <lsb>4</lsb>
       <msb>4</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint14_outtknepdis_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>stsphsercvd</name>
       <description>This interrupt is valid only for Control OUT endpoints and only in Scatter Gather DMA mode. This interrupt is generated only after the core has transferred all the data that the host has sent during the data phase of a control write transfer, to the system memory buffer. The interrupt indicates to the application that the host has switched from data phase to the status phase of a Control Write transfer. The application can use this interrupt to ACK or STALL the Status phase, after it has decoded the data phase. This is applicable only in Case of Scatter Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>5</lsb>
       <msb>5</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint14_stsphsercvd_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>back2backsetup</name>
       <description>Applies to Control OUT endpoints only. This bit indicates that the core has received more than three back-to-back SETUP packets for this particular endpoint. for information about handling this interrupt,</description>
       <lsb>6</lsb>
       <msb>6</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint14_back2backsetup_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>outpkterr</name>
       <description>Applies to OUT endpoints Only This interrupt is asserted when the core detects an overflow or a CRC error for non-Isochronous OUT packet.</description>
       <lsb>8</lsb>
       <msb>8</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint14_outpkterr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bnaintr</name>
       <description>This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled. The core generates this interrupt when the descriptor accessed is not ready for the Core to process, such as Host busy or DMA done</description>
       <lsb>9</lsb>
       <msb>9</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint14_bnaintr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>pktdrpsts</name>
       <description>This bit indicates to the application that an ISOC OUT packet has been dropped. This bit does not have an associated mask bit and does not generate an interrupt.</description>
       <lsb>11</lsb>
       <msb>11</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint14_pktdrpsts_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bbleerr</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when babble is received for the endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>12</lsb>
       <msb>12</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint14_bbleerr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>nakintrpt</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when a NAK is transmitted or received by the device. In case of isochronous IN endpoints the interrupt gets generated when a zero length packet is transmitted due to un-availability of data in the TXFifo.</description>
       <lsb>13</lsb>
       <msb>13</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint14_nakintrpt_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>nyetintrpt</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when a NYET response is transmitted for a non isochronous OUT endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>14</lsb>
       <msb>14</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint14_nyetintrpt_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doeptsiz14</name>
     <description>The application must modify this register before enabling the endpoint. Once the endpoint is enabled using Endpoint Enable bit of the Device Endpoint-n Control registers (DIEPCTLn.EPEna/DOEPCTLn.EPEna), the core modifies this register. The application can only read this register once the core has cleared the Endpoint Enable bit.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xcd0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>xfersize</name>
       <description>Indicates the transfer size in bytes for endpoint 0. The core interrupts the application only after it has exhausted the transfer size amount of data. The transfer size can be Set to the maximum packet size of the endpoint, to be interrupted at the end of each packet. The core decrements this field every time a packet from the external memory is written to the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>18</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>pktcnt</name>
       <description>Indicates the total number of USB packets that constitute the Transfer Size amount of data for endpoint 0.This field is decremented every time a packet (maximum size or short packet) is read from the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>19</lsb>
       <msb>28</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>rxdpid</name>
       <description>Applies to isochronous OUT endpoints only.This is the data PID received in the last packet for this endpoint. Use datax.
Applies to control OUT Endpoints only. Use packetx. This field specifies the number of back-to-back SETUP data packets the endpoint can receive.</description>
       <lsb>29</lsb>
       <msb>30</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doeptsiz14_rxdpid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data0</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data2packet1</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data1packet2</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>mdatapacket3</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdma14</name>
     <description>DMA OUT Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xcd4</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdma14</name>
       <description>Holds the start address of the external memory for storing or fetching endpoint data. for control endpoints, this field stores control OUT data packets as well as SETUP transaction data packets. When more than three SETUP packets are received back-to-back, the SETUP data packet in the memory is overwritten. This register is incremented on every AHB transaction. The application can give only a DWORD-aligned address.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is not enabled, the application programs the start address value in this field.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field indicates the base pointer for the descriptor list.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdmab14</name>
     <description>DMA Buffer Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xcdc</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdmab14</name>
       <description>Holds the current buffer address. This register is updated as and when the data transfer for the corresponding end point is in progress. This register is present only in Scatter/Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepctl15</name>
     <description>Out Endpoint 15.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xce0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>mps</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. The application must program this field with the maximum packet size for the current logical endpoint. This value is in bytes.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>10</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>usbactep</name>
       <description>Indicates whether this endpoint is active in the current configuration and interface. The core clears this bit for all endpoints (other than EP 0) after detecting a USB reset. After receiving the SetConfiguration and SetInterface commands, the application must program endpoint registers accordingly and set this bit.</description>
       <lsb>15</lsb>
       <msb>15</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl15_usbactep_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>dpid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Contains the PID of the packet to be received or transmitted on this endpoint. The application must program the PID of the first packet to be received or transmitted on this endpoint, after the endpoint is activated. The applications use the SetD1PID and SetD0PID fields of this register to program either DATA0 or DATA1 PID.
0: DATA0
1: DATA1This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode. Even/Odd (Micro)Frame (EO_FrNum) In non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode:
Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Indicates the (micro)frame number in which the core transmits/receives isochronous data for this endpoint. The application must program the even/odd (micro) frame number in which it intends to transmit/receive isochronous data for this endpoint using the SetEvnFr and SetOddFr fields in this register.
0: Even (micro)frame
1: Odd (micro)frame
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field is reserved. The frame number in which to send data is provided in the transmit descriptor structure. The frame in which data is received is updated in receive descriptor structure.</description>
       <lsb>16</lsb>
       <msb>16</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl15_dpid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>naksts</name>
       <description>When either the application or the core sets this bit:
-The core stops receiving any data on an OUT endpoint, even if there is space in the RxFIFO to accommodate the incoming packet.
-for non-isochronous IN endpoints: The core stops transmitting any data on an IN endpoint, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
-for isochronous IN endpoints: The core sends out a zero-length data packet, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
       <lsb>17</lsb>
       <msb>17</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl15_naksts_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>nonnak</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>nak</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>eptype</name>
       <description>This is the transfer type supported by this logical endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>18</lsb>
       <msb>19</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl15_eptype_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>control</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>isochronous</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>bulk</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>interrup</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>snp</name>
       <description>Applies to OUT endpoints only. This bit configures the endpoint to Snoop mode. In Snoop mode, the core does not check the correctness of OUT packets before transferring them to application memory.</description>
       <lsb>20</lsb>
       <msb>20</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl15_snp_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disable</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enable</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>stall</name>
       <description>Applies to non-control, non-isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. The application sets this bit to stall all tokens from the USB host to this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Only the application can clear this bit, never the core. Applies to control endpoints only. The application can only set this bit, and the core clears it, when a SETUP token is received for this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
       <lsb>21</lsb>
       <msb>21</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl15_stall_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>cnak</name>
       <description>A write to this bit clears the NAK bit for the endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>26</lsb>
       <msb>26</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl15_cnak_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>snak</name>
       <description>A write to this bit sets the NAK bit for the endpoint. Using this bit, the application can control the transmission of NAK handshakes on an endpoint. The core can also Set this bit for an endpoint after a SETUP packet is received on that endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>27</lsb>
       <msb>27</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl15_snak_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setd0pid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA0. This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode. In non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode: Set Even (micro)frame (SetEvenFr) Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Even/Odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to even (micro) frame. When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field is reserved. The frame number in which to send data is in the transmit descriptor structure. The frame in which to receive data is updated in receive descriptor structure.</description>
       <lsb>28</lsb>
       <msb>28</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl15_setd0pid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setd1pid</name>
       <description>Applies to interrupt/bulk IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA1. This field is applicable both for Scatter/Gather DMA mode and non-Scatter/Gather DMA mode.
Set Odd (micro)frame (SetOddFr) Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only. Writing to this field sets the Even/Odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to odd (micro)frame.This field is not applicable for Scatter/Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>29</lsb>
       <msb>29</msb>
       <access>write-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl15_setd1pid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epdis</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. The application sets this bit to stop transmitting/receiving data on an endpoint, even before the transfer for that endpoint is complete. The application must wait for the Endpoint Disabled interrupt before treating the endpoint as disabled. The core clears this bit before setting the Endpoint Disabled interrupt. The application must set this bit only if Endpoint Enable is already set for this endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>30</lsb>
       <msb>30</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl15_epdis_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epena</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.
-When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled,
-for IN endpoints this bit indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer with data ready to transmit is setup.
-for OUT endpoint it indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer to receive data is setup.
-When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled such as for buffer-pointer based DMA mode:
- for IN endpoints, this bit indicates that data is ready to be transmitted on the endpoint.
- for OUT endpoints, this bit indicates that the application has allocated the memory to start receiving data from the USB.
- The core clears this bit before setting any of the following interrupts on this endpoint:
-SETUP Phase Done
-Endpoint Disabled
-Transfer Completed
for control endpoints in DMA mode, this bit must be set to be able to transfer SETUP data packets in memory.</description>
       <lsb>31</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepctl15_epena_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepint15</name>
     <description>This register indicates the status of an endpoint with respect to USB- and AHB-related events. The application must read this register when the OUT Endpoints Interrupt bit or IN Endpoints Interrupt bit of the Core Interrupt register (GINTSTS.OEPInt or GINTSTS.IEPInt, respectively) is set. Before the application can read this register, it must first read the Device All Endpoints Interrupt (DAINT) register to get the exact endpoint number for the Device Endpoint-n Interrupt register. The application must clear the appropriate bit in this register to clear the corresponding bits in the DAINT and GINTSTS registers.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xce8</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>xfercompl</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled
This field indicates that the requested data from the internal FIFO is moved to external system memory. This interrupt is generated only when the corresponding endpoint descriptor is closed, and the IOC bit for the corresponding descriptor is Set.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is disabled, this field indicates that the programmed transfer is complete on the AHB as well as on the USB, for this endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint15_xfercompl_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>epdisbld</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints. This bit indicates that the endpoint is disabled per the application's request.</description>
       <lsb>1</lsb>
       <msb>1</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint15_epdisbld_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>ahberr</name>
       <description>Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.This is generated only in Internal DMA mode when there is an AHB error during an AHB read/write. The application can read the corresponding endpoint DMA address register to get the error address.</description>
       <lsb>2</lsb>
       <msb>2</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint15_ahberr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>setup</name>
       <description>Applies to control OUT endpoints only. Indicates that the SETUP phase for the control endpoint is complete and no more back-to-back SETUP packets were received for the current control transfer. On this interrupt, the application can decode the received SETUP data packet.</description>
       <lsb>3</lsb>
       <msb>3</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint15_setup_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>outtknepdis</name>
       <description>Applies only to control OUT endpoints. Indicates that an OUT token was received when the endpoint was not yet enabled. This interrupt is asserted on the endpoint for which the OUT token was received.</description>
       <lsb>4</lsb>
       <msb>4</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint15_outtknepdis_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>stsphsercvd</name>
       <description>This interrupt is valid only for Control OUT endpoints and only in Scatter Gather DMA mode. This interrupt is generated only after the core has transferred all the data that the host has sent during the data phase of a control write transfer, to the system memory buffer. The interrupt indicates to the application that the host has switched from data phase to the status phase of a Control Write transfer. The application can use this interrupt to ACK or STALL the Status phase, after it has decoded the data phase. This is applicable only in Case of Scatter Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>5</lsb>
       <msb>5</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint15_stsphsercvd_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>back2backsetup</name>
       <description>Applies to Control OUT endpoints only. This bit indicates that the core has received more than three back-to-back SETUP packets for this particular endpoint. for information about handling this interrupt,</description>
       <lsb>6</lsb>
       <msb>6</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint15_back2backsetup_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>outpkterr</name>
       <description>Applies to OUT endpoints Only This interrupt is asserted when the core detects an overflow or a CRC error for non-Isochronous OUT packet.</description>
       <lsb>8</lsb>
       <msb>8</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint15_outpkterr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bnaintr</name>
       <description>This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled. The core generates this interrupt when the descriptor accessed is not ready for the Core to process, such as Host busy or DMA done</description>
       <lsb>9</lsb>
       <msb>9</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint15_bnaintr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>pktdrpsts</name>
       <description>This bit indicates to the application that an ISOC OUT packet has been dropped. This bit does not have an associated mask bit and does not generate an interrupt.</description>
       <lsb>11</lsb>
       <msb>11</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint15_pktdrpsts_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bbleerr</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when babble is received for the endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>12</lsb>
       <msb>12</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint15_bbleerr_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>nakintrpt</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when a NAK is transmitted or received by the device. In case of isochronous IN endpoints the interrupt gets generated when a zero length packet is transmitted due to un-availability of data in the TXFifo.</description>
       <lsb>13</lsb>
       <msb>13</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint15_nakintrpt_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>nyetintrpt</name>
       <description>The core generates this interrupt when a NYET response is transmitted for a non isochronous OUT endpoint.</description>
       <lsb>14</lsb>
       <msb>14</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doepint15_nyetintrpt_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doeptsiz15</name>
     <description>The application must modify this register before enabling the endpoint. Once the endpoint is enabled using Endpoint Enable bit of the Device Endpoint-n Control registers (DIEPCTLn.EPEna/DOEPCTLn.EPEna), the core modifies this register. The application can only read this register once the core has cleared the Endpoint Enable bit.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xcf0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>xfersize</name>
       <description>Indicates the transfer size in bytes for endpoint 0. The core interrupts the application only after it has exhausted the transfer size amount of data. The transfer size can be Set to the maximum packet size of the endpoint, to be interrupted at the end of each packet. The core decrements this field every time a packet from the external memory is written to the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>18</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>pktcnt</name>
       <description>Indicates the total number of USB packets that constitute the Transfer Size amount of data for endpoint 0.This field is decremented every time a packet (maximum size or short packet) is read from the RxFIFO.</description>
       <lsb>19</lsb>
       <msb>28</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>rxdpid</name>
       <description>Applies to isochronous OUT endpoints only.This is the data PID received in the last packet for this endpoint. Use datax.
Applies to control OUT Endpoints only. Use packetx. This field specifies the number of back-to-back SETUP data packets the endpoint can receive.</description>
       <lsb>29</lsb>
       <msb>30</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_devgrp_doeptsiz15_rxdpid_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data0</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data2packet1</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>data1packet2</name>
          <value>2</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>mdatapacket3</name>
          <value>3</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdma15</name>
     <description>DMA OUT Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xcf4</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdma15</name>
       <description>Holds the start address of the external memory for storing or fetching endpoint data. for control endpoints, this field stores control OUT data packets as well as SETUP transaction data packets. When more than three SETUP packets are received back-to-back, the SETUP data packet in the memory is overwritten. This register is incremented on every AHB transaction. The application can give only a DWORD-aligned address.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is not enabled, the application programs the start address value in this field.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field indicates the base pointer for the descriptor list.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>devgrp_doepdmab15</name>
     <description>DMA Buffer Address.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xcfc</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-only</access>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>doepdmab15</name>
       <description>Holds the current buffer address. This register is updated as and when the data transfer for the corresponding end point is in progress. This register is present only in Scatter/Gather DMA mode.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>31</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>pwrclkgrp_pcgcctl</name>
     <description>This register is available in Host and Device modes. The application can use this register to control the core's power-down and clock gating features. Because the CSR module is turned off during power-down, this register is implemented in the AHB Slave BIU module.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xe00</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>stoppclk</name>
       <description>The application sets this bit to stop the PHY clock (phy_clk) when the USB is suspended, the session is not valid, or the device is disconnected. The application clears this bit when the USB is resumed or a new session starts.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_pwrclkgrp_pcgcctl_stoppclk_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>disabled</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>enabled</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>rstpdwnmodule</name>
       <description>This bit is valid only in Partial Power-Down mode. Theapplication sets this bit when the power is turned off. The application clears this bit after the power is turned on and the PHY clock is up. The R/W of all core registers are possible only when this bit is set to 1b0.</description>
       <lsb>3</lsb>
       <msb>3</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_pwrclkgrp_pcgcctl_rstpdwnmodule_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>on</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>off</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>physleep</name>
       <description>Indicates that the PHY is in Sleep State.</description>
       <lsb>6</lsb>
       <msb>6</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_pwrclkgrp_pcgcctl_physleep_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>l1suspended</name>
       <description>Indicates that the PHY is in deep sleep when in L1 state.</description>
       <lsb>7</lsb>
       <msb>7</msb>
       <access>read-only</access>
       <enumeratedValues>
        <name>usb_pwrclkgrp_pcgcctl_l1suspended_enum</name>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>inactive</name>
          <value>0</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
         <enumeratedValue>
          <name>active</name>
          <value>1</value>
         </enumeratedValue>
       </enumeratedValues>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
   </registers>
  </peripheral>
  <peripheral>
   <name>nandregs</name>
   <version>0.0</version>
   <description>Registers in the NAND Flash Controller module accessible via its register AXI slave</description>
   <disableCondition>(Peripherals-&gt;rstmgr-&gt;rstmgr_permodrst-&gt;nand == 1)</disableCondition><baseAddress>0xffb80000</baseAddress>
   <access>read-write</access>
   <addressBlock>
    <offset>0x0</offset>
    <size>0x7a4</size>
    <usage>registers</usage>
   </addressBlock>
   <registers>
    <register>
     <name>config_device_reset</name>
     <description>Device reset. Controller sends a RESET command to device. Controller resets bit after sending command to device</description>
     <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>bank0</name>
       <description> Issues reset to bank 0. Controller resets the bit after reset command is issued to device. </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bank1</name>
       <description> Issues reset to bank 1. Controller resets the bit after reset command is issued to device. </description>
       <lsb>1</lsb>
       <msb>1</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bank2</name>
       <description> Issues reset to bank 2. Controller resets the bit after reset command is issued to device. </description>
       <lsb>2</lsb>
       <msb>2</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bank3</name>
       <description> Issues reset to bank 3. Controller resets the bit after reset command is issued to device. </description>
       <lsb>3</lsb>
       <msb>3</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_transfer_spare_reg</name>
     <description>Default data transfer mode. (Ignored during Spare only mode)</description>
     <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>flag</name>
       <description>On all read or write commands through Map 01, if this bit is set, data in spare area of memory will be transfered to host along with main area of data. The main area will be transfered followed by spare area.[list][*]1 - MAIN+SPARE [*]0 - MAIN[/list] </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_load_wait_cnt</name>
     <description>Wait count value for Load operation </description>
     <addressOffset>0x20</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x1f4</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description>Number of clock cycles after issue of load operation before NAND Flash Controller polls for status. This values is of relevance for status polling mode of operation and has been provided to minimize redundant polling after issuing a command. After a load command, the first polling will happen after this many number of cycles have elapsed and then on polling will happen every int_mon_cyccnt cycles. The default values is equal to the default value of int_mon_cyccnt </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>15</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_program_wait_cnt</name>
     <description>Wait count value for Program operation </description>
     <addressOffset>0x30</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x1f4</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description>Number of clock cycles after issue of program operation before NAND Flash Controller polls for status. This values is of relevance for status polling mode of operation and has been provided to minimize redundant polling after issuing a command. After a program command, the first polling will happen after this many number of cycles have elapsed and then on polling will happen every int_mon_cyccnt cycles. The default values is equal to the default value of int_mon_cyccnt. The controller internally multiplies the value programmed into this register by 16 to provide a wider range for polling.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>15</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_erase_wait_cnt</name>
     <description>Wait count value for Erase operation</description>
     <addressOffset>0x40</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x1f4</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description>Number of clock cycles after issue of erase operation before NAND Flash Controller polls for status. This values is of relevance for status polling mode of operation and has been provided to minimize redundant polling after issuing a command. After a erase command, the first polling will happen after this many number of cycles have elapsed and then on polling will happen every int_mon_cyccnt cycles. The default values is equal to the default value of int_mon_cyccnt. The controller internally multiplies the value programmed into this register by 16 to provide a wider range for polling.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>15</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_int_mon_cyccnt</name>
     <description>Interrupt monitor cycle count value</description>
     <addressOffset>0x50</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x1f4</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description>In polling mode, sets the number of cycles Denali Flash Controller must wait before checking the status register. This register is only used when R/B pins are not available to NAND Flash Controller. </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>15</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_rb_pin_enabled</name>
     <description>Interrupt or polling mode. Ready/Busy pin is enabled from device.</description>
     <addressOffset>0x60</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x1</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>bank0</name>
       <description>Sets Denali Flash Controller in interrupt pin or polling mode [list][*]1 - R/B pin enabled for bank 0. Interrupt pin mode. [*]0 - R/B pin disabled for bank 0. Polling mode.[/list]</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bank1</name>
       <description>Sets Denali Flash Controller in interrupt pin or polling mode [list][*]1 - R/B pin enabled for bank 1. Interrupt pin mode. [*]0 - R/B pin disabled for bank 1. Polling mode.[/list]</description>
       <lsb>1</lsb>
       <msb>1</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bank2</name>
       <description>Sets Denali Flash Controller in interrupt pin or polling mode [list][*]1 - R/B pin enabled for bank 2. Interrupt pin mode. [*]0 - R/B pin disabled for bank 2. Polling mode.[/list]</description>
       <lsb>2</lsb>
       <msb>2</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>bank3</name>
       <description>Sets Denali Flash Controller in interrupt pin or polling mode [list][*]1 - R/B pin enabled for bank 3. Interrupt pin mode. [*]0 - R/B pin disabled for bank 3. Polling mode.[/list]</description>
       <lsb>3</lsb>
       <msb>3</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_multiplane_operation</name>
     <description>Multiplane transfer mode. Pipelined read, copyback, erase and program commands are transfered in multiplane mode</description>
     <addressOffset>0x70</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>flag</name>
       <description>[list][*]1 - Multiplane operation enabled [*]0 - Multiplane operation disabled[/list]</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_multiplane_read_enable</name>
     <description>Device supports multiplane read command sequence</description>
     <addressOffset>0x80</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>flag</name>
       <description>Certain devices support dedicated multiplane read command sequences to read data in the same fashion as is written with multiplane program commands. This bit set should be set for the above devices. When not set, pipeline reads in multiplane mode will still happen in the order of multiplane writes, though normal read command sequences will be issued to the device. [list][*]1 - Device supports multiplane read sequence [*]0 - Device does not support multiplane read sequence[/list] </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_copyback_disable</name>
     <description>Device does not support copyback command sequence </description>
     <addressOffset>0x90</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>flag</name>
       <description>[list][*]1 - Copyback disabled [*]0 - Copyback enabled[/list] </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_cache_write_enable</name>
     <description>Device supports cache write command sequence </description>
     <addressOffset>0xa0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>flag</name>
       <description>[list][*]1 - Cache write supported [*]0 - Cache write not supported[/list] </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_cache_read_enable</name>
     <description>Device supports cache read command sequence </description>
     <addressOffset>0xb0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>flag</name>
       <description>[list][*]1 - Cache read supported [*]0 - Cache read not supported[/list] </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_prefetch_mode</name>
     <description>Enables read data prefetching to faster performance </description>
     <addressOffset>0xc0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x1</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>prefetch_en</name>
       <description>Enable prefetch of Data</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>prefetch_burst_length</name>
       <description>If prefetch_en is set and prefetch_burst_length is set to ZERO, the controller will start prefetching data only after the receiving the first Map01 read command for the page. If prefetch_en is set and prefetch_burst_length is set to a non-ZERO, valid value, the controller will start prefetching data corresponding to this value even before the first Map01 for the current page has been received. The value written here should be in bytes. </description>
       <lsb>4</lsb>
       <msb>15</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_chip_enable_dont_care</name>
     <description>Device can work in the chip enable dont care mode </description>
     <addressOffset>0xd0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>flag</name>
       <description>Controller can interleave commands between banks when this feature is enabled. [list][*]1 - Device in dont care mode [*]0 - Device cares for chip enable[/list] </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_ecc_enable</name>
     <description>Enable controller ECC check bit generation and correction </description>
     <addressOffset>0xe0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x1</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>flag</name>
       <description>Enables or disables controller ECC capabilities. When enabled, controller calculates ECC check-bits and writes them onto device on program operation. On page reads, check-bits are recomputed and errors reported, if any, after comparing with stored check-bits. When disabled, controller does not compute check-bits. [list][*]1 - ECC Enabled [*]0 - ECC disabled[/list] </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_global_int_enable</name>
     <description>Global Interrupt enable and Error/Timeout disable.</description>
     <addressOffset>0xf0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>flag</name>
       <description>Host will receive an interrupt only when this bit is set.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>timeout_disable</name>
       <description>Watchdog timer logic will be de-activated when this bit is set.</description>
       <lsb>4</lsb>
       <msb>4</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>error_rpt_disable</name>
       <description>Command and ECC uncorrectable failures will not be reported when this bit is set </description>
       <lsb>8</lsb>
       <msb>8</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_twhr2_and_we_2_re</name>
     <addressOffset>0x100</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x1432</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>we_2_re</name>
       <description>Signifies the number of bus interface nand_mp_clk clocks that should be introduced between write enable going high to read enable going low. The number of clocks is the function of device parameter Twhr and controller clock frequency. </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>5</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>twhr2</name>
       <description>Signifies the number of controller clocks that should be introduced between the last command of a random data output command to the start of the data transfer. </description>
       <lsb>8</lsb>
       <msb>13</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_tcwaw_and_addr_2_data</name>
     <addressOffset>0x110</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x1432</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>addr_2_data</name>
       <description>Signifies the number of bus interface nand_mp_clk clocks that should be introduced between address latch enable going low to write enable going low. The number of clocks is the function of device parameter Tadl and controller clock frequency. </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>5</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
      <field>
       <name>tcwaw</name>
       <description>Signifies the number of controller clocks that should be introduced between the command cycle of a random data input command to the address cycle of the random data input command. </description>
       <lsb>8</lsb>
       <msb>13</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_re_2_we</name>
     <description>Timing parameter between re high to we low (Trhw) </description>
     <addressOffset>0x120</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x32</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description>Signifies the number of bus interface nand_mp_clk clocks that should be introduced between read enable going high to write enable going low. The number of clocks is the function of device parameter Trhw and controller clock frequency. </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>5</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_acc_clks</name>
     <description>Timing parameter from read enable going low to capture read data </description>
     <addressOffset>0x130</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description>Signifies the number of bus interface nand_mp_clk clock cycles, controller should wait from read enable going low to sending out a strobe of nand_mp_clk for capturing of incoming data. </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>3</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_number_of_planes</name>
     <description>Number of planes in the device </description>
     <addressOffset>0x140</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description>Controller will read Electronic Signature of devices and populate this field as the number of planes information is present in the signature. For 512B device, this information needs to be programmed by software. Software could also choose to override the populated value. The values in the fields should be as follows[list] [*]3'h0 - Monoplane device [*]3'h1 - Two plane device [*]3'h3 - 4 plane device [*]3'h7 - 8 plane device [*]All other values - Reserved[/list] </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>2</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_pages_per_block</name>
     <description>Number of pages in a block </description>
     <addressOffset>0x150</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description>Controller will read Electronic Signature of devices and populate this field. The PAGE512 field of the System Manager NANDGRP_BOOTSTRAP register will determine the value of this field to be of 32. Software could also choose to override the populated value. </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>15</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_device_width</name>
     <description>I/O width of attached devices </description>
     <addressOffset>0x160</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x3</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description>Controller will read Electronic Signature of devices and populate this field. Software could also choose to override the populated value although only one value is supported. The values in this field should be as follows[list][*]2'h00 - 8bit device[*]All other values - Reserved[/list] </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>1</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_device_main_area_size</name>
     <description>Page main area size of device in bytes </description>
     <addressOffset>0x170</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description>Controller will read Electronic Signature of devices and populate this field. The PAGE512 field of the System Manager NANDGRP_BOOTSTRAP register will determine the value of this field to be 512. Software could also choose to override the populated value.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>15</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_device_spare_area_size</name>
     <description>Page spare area size of device in bytes </description>
     <addressOffset>0x180</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description>Controller will read Electronic Signature of devices and populate this field. The PAGE512 field of the System Manager NANDGRP_BOOTSTRAP register will determine the value of this field to be 16. Software could also choose to override the populated value.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>15</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_two_row_addr_cycles</name>
     <description>Attached device has only 2 ROW address cycles </description>
     <addressOffset>0x190</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>flag</name>
       <description>This flag must be set for devices which allow for 2 ROW address cycles instead of the usual 3. Alternatively, the TWOROWADDR field of the System Manager NANDGRP_BOOTSTRAP register when asserted will set this flag.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_multiplane_addr_restrict</name>
     <description>Address restriction for multiplane commands </description>
     <addressOffset>0x1a0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>flag</name>
       <description>This flag must be set for devices which require that during multiplane operations all but the address for the last plane should have their address cycles tied low. The last plane address cycles has proper values. This ensures multiplane address restrictions in the device.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_ecc_correction</name>
     <description>Correction capability required </description>
     <addressOffset>0x1b0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x8</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description>The required correction capability can be a number less than the configured error correction capability. A smaller correction capability will lead to lesser number of ECC check-bits being written per ECC sector. </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>7</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_read_mode</name>
     <description>The type of read sequence that the controller will follow for pipe read commands. </description>
     <addressOffset>0x1c0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description> The values in the field should be as follows[list] [*]4'h0 - This value informs the controller that the pipe read sequence to follow is of a normal read. For 512 byte page devices, Normal read sequence is, C00, Address, Data, ..... For devices with page size greater that 512 bytes, the sequence is, C00, Address, C30, Data..... [*]4'h1 - This value informs the controller that the pipe read sequence to follow is of a Cache Read with the following sequence, C00, Address, C30, C31, Data, C31, Data, ....., C3F, Data. [*]4'h2 - This value informs the controller that the pipe read sequence to follow is of a Cache Read with the following sequence, C00, Address, C31, Data, Data, ....., C34. [*]4'h3 - This value informs the controller that the pipe read sequence to follow is of a 'N' Plane Read with the following sequence, C00, Address, C00, Address, C30, Data, C06, Address, CE0, Data..... [*]4'h4 - This value informs the controller that the pipe read sequence to follow is of a 'N' Plane Read with the following sequence, C60, Address, C60, Address, C30, C00, Address, C05, Address, CE0, Data, C00, Address, C05, Address, CE0, Data..... [*]4'h5 - This value informs the controller that the pipe read sequence to follow is of a 'N' Plane Cache Read with the following sequence, C60, Address, C60, Address, C30, C31, C00, Address, C05, Address, CE0, Data, C00, Address, C05, Address, CE0, Data, ....., C3F, C00, Address, C05, Address, CE0, Data, C00, Address, C05, Address, CE0, Data [*]4'h6 - This value informs the controller that the pipe read sequence to follow is of a 'N' Plane Read with the following sequence, C00, Address, C32, .., C00, Address, C30, C06, Address, CE0, Data, C06, Address, CE0, Data,.... [*]4'h7 - This value informs the controller that the pipe read sequence to follow is of a 'N' Plane Cache Read with the following sequence, C00, Address, C32,..., C00, Address, C30, C31,C06, Address, CE0, Data, C31, C06, Address, CE0, Data, C3F, C06, Address, CE0, Data.... [*]4'h8 - This value informs the controller that the pipe read sequence to follow is of a 'N' Plane Cache Read with the following sequence, C60, Address, C60, Address, C33, C31, C00, Address, C05, Address, CE0, Data, C00, Address, C05, Address, CE0, Data, ....., C3F, C00, Address, C05, Address, CE0, Data, C00, Address, C05, Address, CE0, Data [*]4'h9 - 4'h15 - Reserved. [/list] ..... indicates that the previous sequence is repeated till the last page. </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>3</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_write_mode</name>
     <description>The type of write sequence that the controller will follow for pipe write commands. </description>
     <addressOffset>0x1d0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description>The values in the field should be as follows[list] [*]4'h0 - This value informs the controller that the pipe write sequence to follow is of a normal write with the following sequence, C80, Address, Data, C10..... [*]4'h1 - This value informs the controller that the pipe write sequence to follow is of a Cache Program with the following sequence, C80, Address, Data, C15, ....., C80, Address, Data, C10. [*]4'h2 - This value informs the controller that the pipe write sequence to follow is of a Two/Four Plane Program with the following sequence, C80, Address, Data, C11, C81, Address, Data, C10..... [*]4'h3 - This value informs the controller that the pipe write sequence to follow is of a 'N' Plane Program with the following sequence, C80, Address, Data, C11, C80, Address, Data, C10..... [*]4'h4 - This value informs the controller that the pipe write sequence to follow is of a 'N' Plane Cache Program with the following sequence, C80, Address, Data, C11, C80, Address, Data, C15.....C80, Address, Data, C11, C80, Address, Data, C10. [*]4'h5 - This value informs the controller that the pipe write sequence to follow is of a 'N' Plane Cache Program with the following sequence, C80, Address, Data, C11, C81, Address, Data, C15.....C80, Address, Data, C11, C81, Address, Data, C10. [*]4'h6 - 4'h15 - Reserved. [/list] ..... indicates that the previous sequence is repeated till the last page. </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>3</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_copyback_mode</name>
     <description>The type of copyback sequence that the controller will follow. </description>
     <addressOffset>0x1e0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description>The values in the field should be as follows[list] [*]4'h0 - This value informs the controller that the copyback sequence to follow is, C00, Address, C35, C85, Address, C10 [*]4'h1 - This value informs the controller that the copyback sequence to follow is, C00, Address, C30, C8C, Address, C10 [*]4'h2 - This value informs the controller that the copyback sequence to follow is, C00, Address, C8A, Address, C10 [*]4'h3 - This value informs the controller that the copyback sequence to follow is of a four plane copyback sequence, C00, Address, C03, Address, C03, Address, C03, Address, C8A, Address, C11, C8A, Address, C11, C8A, Address, C11, C8A, Address, C10. [*]4'h4 - This value informs the controller that the copyback sequence to follow is of a two plane copyback sequence, C00, Address, C35, C00, Address, C35, C85, Address, C11, C81, Address, C10. [*]4'h5 - This value informs the controller that the copyback sequence to follow is of a two plane copyback sequence, C60, Address, C60, Address, C35, C85, Address, C11, C81, Address, C10. [*]4'h6 - This value informs the controller that the copyback sequence to follow is of a two plane copyback sequence, C00, Address, C00, Address, C35, C85, Address, C11, C80, Address, C10. [*]4'h7 - This value informs the controller that the copyback sequence to follow is of a two plane copyback sequence, C60, Address, C60, Address, C30, C8C, Address, C11, C8C, Address, C10. [*]4'h8 - 4'h15 - Reserved.[/list] </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>3</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_rdwr_en_lo_cnt</name>
     <description>Read/Write Enable low pulse width </description>
     <addressOffset>0x1f0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x12</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description>Number of nand_mp_clk cycles that read or write enable will kept low to meet the min Trp/Twp parameter of the device. The value in this register plus rdwr_en_hi_cnt register value should meet the min cycle time of the device connected. The default value is calculated assuming the max nand_mp_clk time period of 4ns to work with ONFI Mode 0 mode of 100ns device cycle time. This assumes a 1x/4x clocking scheme. </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>4</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_rdwr_en_hi_cnt</name>
     <description>Read/Write Enable high pulse width </description>
     <addressOffset>0x200</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0xc</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description>Number of nand_mp_clk cycles that read or write enable will kept high to meet the min Treh/Tweh parameter of the device. The value in this register plus rdwr_en_lo_cnt register value should meet the min cycle time of the device connected. The default value is calculated assuming the max nand_mp_clk time period of 4ns to work with ONFI Mode 0 mode of 100ns device cycle time. This assumes a 1x/4x clocking scheme. </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>4</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_max_rd_delay</name>
     <description>Max round trip read data delay for data capture </description>
     <addressOffset>0x210</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description>Number of nand_mp_clk cycles after generation of feedback nand_mp_clk pulse when it is safe to synchronize received data to nand_mp_clk domain. Data should have been registered with nand_mp_clk and stable by the time max_rd_delay cycles has elapsed. A default value of zero will mean a value of nand_mp_clk multiple minus one. </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>3</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_cs_setup_cnt</name>
     <description>Chip select setup time </description>
     <addressOffset>0x220</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x3</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description>Number of nand_mp_clk cycles required for meeting chip select setup time. This register refers to device timing parameter Tcs. The value in this registers reflects the extra setup cycles for chip select before read/write enable signal is set low. The default value is calculated for ONFI Timing mode 0 Tcs = 70ns and maximum nand_mp_clk period of 4ns for 1x/4x clock multiple for 16ns cycle time device. Please refer to Figure 3.3 for the relationship between the cs_setup_cnt and rdwr_en_lo_cnt values.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>4</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_spare_area_skip_bytes</name>
     <description>Spare area skip bytes </description>
     <addressOffset>0x230</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description>Number of bytes to skip from start of spare area before last ECC sector data starts. The bytes will be written with the value programmed in the spare_area_marker register. This register could be potentially used to preserve the bad block marker in the spare area by marking it good. The default value is zero which means no bytes will be skipped and last ECC sector will start from the beginning of spare area. </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>5</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_spare_area_marker</name>
     <description>Spare area marker value </description>
     <addressOffset>0x240</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0xffff</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description>The value that will be written in the spare area skip bytes. This value will be used by controller while in the MAIN mode of data transfer. Only the least-significant 8 bits of the field value are used.</description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>15</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_devices_connected</name>
     <description>Number of Devices connected on one bank </description>
     <addressOffset>0x250</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description>Indicates the number of devices connected to a bank. At reset, the value loaded is the maximum possible devices that could be connected in this configuration. </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>2</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_die_mask</name>
     <description>Indicates the die differentiator in case of NAND devices with stacked dies. </description>
     <addressOffset>0x260</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description>The die_mask register information will be used for devices having address restrictions. For example, in certain Samsung devices, when the first address in a two-plane command is being sent, it is expected that the address is all zeros. But if the NAND device internally has multiple dies stacked, the die information (MSB of final row address) has to be sent. The value programmed in this register will be used to mask the address while sending out the last row address. </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>7</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_first_block_of_next_plane</name>
     <description>The starting block address of the next plane in a multi plane device.</description>
     <addressOffset>0x270</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x1</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description>This values informs the controller of the plane structure of the device. In case the device is a multi plane device and the value here is 1, the controller understands that the next plane starts from Block number 1 and in conjunction with the number of planes parameter can decide upon the distribution of blocks in a plane in the device. </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>15</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_write_protect</name>
     <description>This register is used to control the assertion/de-assertion of the WP# pin to the device. </description>
     <addressOffset>0x280</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x1</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>flag</name>
       <description> When the controller is in reset, the WP# pin is always asserted to the device. Once the reset is removed, the WP# is de-asserted. The software will then have to come and program this bit to assert/de-assert the same. [list][*]1 - Write protect de-assert [*]0 - Write protect assert[/list] </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>0</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_re_2_re</name>
     <description>Timing parameter between re high to re low (Trhz) for the next bank </description>
     <addressOffset>0x290</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x32</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description>Signifies the number of bus interface nand_mp_clk clocks that should be introduced between read enable going high to a bank to the read enable going low to the next bank. The number of clocks is the function of device parameter Trhz and controller clock frequency. </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>5</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_por_reset_count</name>
     <description>The number of cycles the controller waits after reset to issue the first RESET command to the device. </description>
     <addressOffset>0x2a0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x13b</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description>The controller waits for this number of cycles before issuing the first RESET command to the device. The number in this register is multiplied internally by 16 in the controller to form the final reset wait count. </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>15</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>config_watchdog_reset_count</name>
     <description>The number of cycles the controller waits before flagging a watchdog timeout interrupt. </description>
     <addressOffset>0x2b0</addressOffset>
     <size>32</size>
     <access>read-write</access>
     <resetValue>0x5b9a</resetValue>
     <resetMask>0xffffffff</resetMask>
     <fields>
      <field>
       <name>value</name>
       <description>The controller waits for this number of cycles before issuing a watchdog timeout interrupt. The value in this register is multiplied internally by 32 in the controller to form the final watchdog counter. </description>
       <lsb>0</lsb>
       <msb>15</msb>
       <access>read-write</access>
      </field>
     </fields>
    </register>
    <register>
     <name>param_manufacturer_id</name>
     <addressOffset>0x300</addressOffset>
